// The collections package: a growable list, an owned string, a dictionary of
// any keys that are Hash (and an ordered one, which visits in insertion order),
// a set, and a double-ended queue, each holding its elements in one block of memory
// that it gets, grows and gives back through a region it points to (core's
// 'MemRegion'; the heap's, which calls the C allocator, unless it is made in
// another). Built as Congo needs them, and no further.
//
//   mod names;
//   import collections use *;
//
//   fn main() {
//     mut seen = List[String]() <- String.from("core");
//     seen.push(String.from("stdio"));
//     mut counts = Dict[String, i64]() <- String.from("core"): 1;
//     if counts.contains("core") { ... *counts["core"] ... }
//   }
//
// Each collection is a region in the type sense: the value itself is small (a
// pointer to its block, a count, a capacity, and the region the block comes
// from) and lives wherever it is put -- on the stack, in a field, in another
// collection, or behind a 'Uni' or 'Rc' owner -- while its elements live inline
// in the block it owns. When the collection dies, its 'final' finalizes each
// element still in it, first to last, and then gives the block back to its
// region. Nothing here goes through 'Uni' or 'Rc'.
//
// THE REGION. Each type here declares 'CarriesRegion' (a provisional name), the
// compiler's word for a type that gets its memory from a region it is handed
// when it is made. The region is a '*MemRegion', core's four functions to
// allocate, resize and release a block and to say how much room a block of a
// given size has, held as a collection's last field
// ('region'), and every block comes from it and goes back to it. A construction
// that names none is handed the heap's ('heapRegion()': the raw blocks layer's
// 'alloc', 'resize' and 'release', a static object that is never null), so 'List[i64]()' and
// 'List[i64].empty()' behave as they always have. Another is passed as the
// construction's last argument, 'List[i64](100, r)', where 'r' is a '*MemRegion'.
// A collection holding others hands them its own region where they are made
// inside it (a String its List, a Set its Dict), and a block no region can own
// -- what 'freeze' hands to a 'Uni', which gives it back to the raw blocks layer -- is
// copied to the heap when the region is not the heap's.
//
// Moving values in and out of the block is done with core's 'mem' intrinsics:
// 'mem.writeRaw' moves a value into a slot that holds nothing live,
// 'mem.readRaw' moves one out and leaves the slot dead, and 'mem.finalize'
// runs a value's death where it sits. That is why a slot beyond the count
// never has its garbage released or finalized.
//
// Borrowed references, for now: a collection lends out borrows only for brief
// reading. 'list[i]' and 'dict[key]' give a '&' to one element, and 'view()'
// a slice of the whole, to read at once and let go of; '&mut list[i]' and
// 'viewMut()' lend the same for changing in place ('each x in list.mutItems()'
// walks the same slice, 'x' a '&mut' of each; 'each x in list.drain()' moves
// the values out one by one and leaves the list empty), and while one is used the
// collection may not be touched any other way. Nothing here stores a
// borrow, and nothing a collection returns should be kept across a change to
// it: a push can move the whole block. The compiler catches that for a
// collection held in a local -- while 'imm e = list[0usize]' is still used,
// 'list' may be read but not pushed onto -- and the same for one reached
// through a shared path (a '&mut List' parameter, a field of 'self', a
// 'Rc[mut, T]' owner), against a push through the same name; a push through
// another reference to it is not seen, and leaves 'e' dangling.
// Each type here declares 'ShapeChanging' (a provisional name), which makes
// a borrow its methods return freeze the path it came through. Where a borrow would
// have been the natural return
// (a trimmed piece of a string, the lines of a text), the collection returns
// owned copies instead. (A dictionary's 'get' and its cursors 'keys', 'values'
// and 'entries' do lend borrows, and the compiler keeps a dictionary in a
// local loaned while one is used.)
//
// Threads: List, Deque, Dict, OrderedDict and Set declare 'Sendable', so a collection may be moved
// to another thread, or shared as 'Arc[imm, T]', where its elements may. The
// declaration is a promise the compiler takes on trust for the block's raw
// pointer, which it cannot check; an instance is Sendable only where its type
// arguments are, so a List[&mut Point] is not. A String is Sendable by its
// List[u8]. Nothing here is safe to change from two threads at once.
//
// A collection is constructed with 'new': 'List[i64]()' has no block yet,
// 'List[i64](100)' room for at least 100 (exactly 100 in the heap's region, and in
// any region that gives what is asked; a region that rounds a block up gives a
// List and a Deque all of the room), and the values it starts with
// follow '<-', each appended in turn: 'List[i64](100) <- fill 0', 'new
// Dict[String, i64] <- String.from("a"): 1' (a dictionary's '<-' takes a key
// and a value, and inserts them). Every collection takes a starting size the
// same way: 'Deque[i64](100)', 'String(100)' (bytes, and the NUL after
// them), and for the tables, 'Dict[String, i64](100)', 'new
// OrderedDict[String, i64](100)' and 'Set[String](100)', room for 100
// entries without growing (rounded up to the table's size), as is 'new
// Pool[Npc](100)' for its slots. A function a type offers without a receiver
// makes one too: 'List[i64].empty()', 'List[i64].withCapacity(n)' (never less
// than 4), 'String.from("text")', and a 'withCapacity(n)' on each of the others.
//
// Methods take their receiver as '&' or '&mut'; calling one on a variable
// borrows the variable for the call ('list.push(x)' is '(&mut list).push(x)').
//
// Every method of a generic type is compiled for every instance, whether it is
// called or not, so no method here may assume anything of an element type that
// a move-only type such as String would fail: a list has no 'get' copying an
// element out (a dictionary's 'getCopy' exists only where its value type
// copies). Read one through 'list[i]'; move one out with 'pop' or 'remove'.
// An element left inside an Option one of them returns is finalized with it.
//
// An index out of range, or a key a dictionary does not hold, is a program
// error, and a panic.
//
// What a collection can do is named by a few small traits at the end of this file,
// 'Collection', 'Indexed', 'Stack', 'Queue' and 'Map', which the
// collections meet by their methods' names alone.

mod collections;

import libc;

// A String's own method is named 'cstr', which hides the type of that name
// inside String's declaration; this is the type, for there
alias CStr = cstr;

// -------- the region --------

// Every block a collection holds comes from its region and goes back to it:
// these are the three calls, for a block of n values of T (or, for the tables
// that keep their parts in one block, of bytes)
fn blockNew[T](r: *MemRegion, n: usize) *T inline {
  (r as &MemRegion).allocate(n * mem.sizeof[T](), mem.alignof[T]()) as *T
}

fn blockGrow[T](r: *MemRegion, block: *T, old: usize, want: usize) *T inline {
  (r as &MemRegion).resize(block as *u8, old * mem.sizeof[T](), want * mem.sizeof[T](), mem.alignof[T]()) as *T
}

fn blockFree[T](r: *MemRegion, block: *T, n: usize) inline {
  (r as &MemRegion).release(block as *u8, n * mem.sizeof[T]());
}

// How many values of T a block asked for n of them really holds: the region's
// size for those bytes (core's 'MemRegion.sizeFor'; the room the block will have,
// which an exact region makes the bytes asked for), as a whole number of values.
// A collection that grows by capacity asks this before it allocates, and takes the
// answer as its capacity. It is at least n, and it is the count 'blockGrow' and
// 'blockFree' are given back: the region finds the block's size from the bytes of
// a whole number of values that fit in its room. (List, String and Deque ask. The
// tables of Dict, OrderedDict and Set are a power of two of buckets whose entries
// and control bytes share one block, so the room beyond it is not theirs to use.)
fn blockRoom[T](r: *MemRegion, n: usize) usize inline {
  let size = mem.sizeof[T]();
  if size == 0usize {n} else {(r as &MemRegion).sizeFor(n * size, mem.alignof[T]()) / size}
}

// Whether a region is the heap's, whose blocks the raw blocks layer gives back
// (as an owner's death does)
fn isHeap(r: *MemRegion) bool inline { r as usize == heapRegion() as usize }

// -------- List --------

// A growable list of T, in one block of 'cap' slots of which the first 'used'
// hold live values. Pushing grows it 4, 8, 16, ...; it never shrinks, and
// gives its block back only when it dies. The block comes from the list's
// region, the last field.
pub struct List[T] is ShapeChanging, Sendable, CarriesRegion, GcTraceElements {
  ptr: *T;
  used: usize;
  cap: usize;
  region: *MemRegion;

  // The methods of 'Array[T]', the body 'view' lends, are the list's too, as
  // 'str''s are a String's: its 'len', written once on the body
  use Array[T] via view but iter, mutItems, mutChunks, mutArrays;

  // An empty list, 'List[i32]()': no block until the first value arrives. The
  // last argument of every init is the region the list's block will come from,
  // which a construction that names none leaves out and is handed the heap's.
  pub fn initEmpty overload init(self: &new, region: *MemRegion) { *self = List(null, 0usize, 0usize, region); }

  // An empty list whose block has room for at least n values, 'List[i32](100)',
  // which is what its contents' 'fill' fills: 'List[i32](100) <- fill 0'. The
  // region is asked how much room a block of n values has before the block is
  // made, and the list's capacity is all of it: exactly n where the region gives
  // what is asked (the heap's does), more where the region rounds up.
  pub fn initCapacity overload init(self: &new, n: usize, region: *MemRegion) {
    *self = List(null, 0usize, 0usize, region);
    if n > 0usize {
      let room = blockRoom[T](region, n);
      self.ptr = blockNew[T](region, n);
      self.cap = room;
    }
  }

  // An empty list: no block until the first value arrives
  pub fn empty() List { List(null, 0usize, 0usize, heapRegion()) }

  // An empty list whose block already has room for n values
  pub fn withCapacity(n: usize) List {
    var l = List(null, 0usize, 0usize, heapRegion());
    l.reserve(n);
    l
  }

  // How many values it holds ('len', the body's), and how many its block has
  // room for
  pub fn capacity(self: &) usize { cap }
  pub fn isEmpty(self: &) bool { used == 0usize }

  // Make room for 'more' values beyond those it holds. A block too small is
  // replaced by one twice its size, or exactly big enough if that is more,
  // and never smaller than 4 (and the capacity is then all the room the region
  // gave the block, which may be more). The block may move: every borrow into
  // it, and every slice from 'view', is then stale.
  pub fn reserve(self: &mut, more: usize) {
    if used + more <= cap { return; }
    var want = cap * 2usize;
    if want < used + more { want = used + more; }
    if want < 4usize { want = 4usize; }
    let room = blockRoom[T](region, want);
    ptr = blockGrow[T](region, ptr, cap, want);
    cap = room;
  }

  // Move a value in at the end
  pub fn push(self: &mut, value: T) {
    self.reserve(1usize);
    mem.writeRaw(ptr + used, value);
    used = used + 1usize;
  }

  // 'list <- value' is 'list.push(value)'
  pub fn append overload `<-`(self: &mut, value: T) { self.push(value); }

  // Add a copy of every value of src at the end, in order, with one reserve
  // and one block copy (a memmove of the whole run), where 'push' in a loop
  // reserves and writes one at a time. src is any slice: another list's
  // 'view()', an array, a part of either. Only a type whose values copy can
  // be added this way, as for 'resize'; a list of a move type takes the values
  // of another list with 'appendList' instead. The block may move, as for
  // 'reserve', so a borrow into this list is stale afterwards. src may not be a
  // slice of this very list: the compiler refuses 'list.appendSlice(list.view())'
  // (1168), since the block may move under the slice being copied.
  pub fn appendSlice(self: &mut, src: &Array[T]) where T is Copy {
    let n = src.len;
    if n == 0usize { return; }
    self.reserve(n);
    mem.moveRaw(ptr + used, src as *T, n);
    used = used + n;
  }

  // Move every value of another list to the end of this one, in order, with
  // one reserve and one block move; the other list is consumed, its block
  // given back, and no value is copied or finalized, so a move type (a list
  // of Strings) is appended this way. A list of a copy type that is to be
  // kept uses 'appendSlice(other.view())'.
  pub fn appendList(self: &mut, other: List[T]) {
    var from = other;
    let n = from.used;
    if n == 0usize { return; }
    self.reserve(n);
    mem.moveRaw(ptr + used, from.ptr, n);
    used = used + n;
    from.used = 0usize;
  }

  // Move the last value out, or None when it is empty
  pub fn pop(self: &mut) Option[T] {
    if used == 0usize { return None; }
    used = used - 1usize;
    Some[T](mem.readRaw(ptr + used))
  }

  // A borrow of the last value, for reading it now, or None when it is empty:
  // what 'pop' would move out, left where it is (the Stack trait's 'peek')
  pub fn peek(self: &) Option[&T] {
    if used == 0usize { return None; }
    Some[&T]((ptr + (used - 1usize)) as &T)
  }

  // A borrow of the value at index, or None when index is out of range: the
  // lookup that does not panic (the Indexed trait's 'get')
  pub fn get(self: &, index: usize) Option[&T] {
    if index >= used { return None; }
    Some[&T]((ptr + index) as &T)
  }

  // A borrow of the value at index, for reading it now. Each of these four
  // reports an index out of range at the line of its caller, which its
  // 'file' and 'line' default to
  pub fn `[]`(self: &, index: usize, file: &Array[u8] = srcFile(), line: u32 = srcLine()) &T {
    if index >= used { outOfRange(index, used, file, line); }
    (ptr + index) as &T
  }

  // A mutable borrow of the value at index, for changing it in place:
  // '&mut list[i]'
  pub fn `&[]`(self: &mut, index: usize, file: &Array[u8] = srcFile(), line: u32 = srcLine()) &mut T {
    if index >= used { outOfRange(index, used, file, line); }
    (ptr + index) as &mut T
  }

  // Put value at index, finalizing the value it replaces
  pub fn set(self: &mut, index: usize, value: T, file: &Array[u8] = srcFile(), line: u32 = srcLine()) {
    if index >= used { outOfRange(index, used, file, line); }
    mem.finalize(ptr + index);
    mem.writeRaw(ptr + index, value);
  }

  // Exchange the values at two indexes
  pub fn swap(self: &mut, a: usize, b: usize, file: &Array[u8] = srcFile(), line: u32 = srcLine()) {
    if a >= used { outOfRange(a, used, file, line); }
    if b >= used { outOfRange(b, used, file, line); }
    if a == b { return; }
    let held = mem.readRaw(ptr + a);
    mem.moveRaw(ptr + a, ptr + b, 1usize);
    mem.writeRaw(ptr + b, held);
  }

  // Finalize every value, first to last, and keep the block for reuse
  pub fn clear(self: &mut) {
    self.finalizeAll();
    used = 0usize;
  }

  // Shorten it to n values, finalizing each one dropped, first to last, and
  // keeping the block. A list of n values or fewer is left as it is.
  pub fn truncate(self: &mut, n: usize) {
    if n >= used { return; }
    if mem.needsFinal[T]() {
      var i = n;
      while i < used { mem.finalize(ptr + i); i = i + 1usize; }
    }
    used = n;
  }

  // Make it exactly n values long. A longer list is truncated: the values
  // beyond n are finalized, first to last. A shorter one grows, each new value
  // a copy of 'value', and the block may move, as for 'reserve'. Only a type
  // whose values copy can fill the new places, so a type with a finalizer
  // (a move type) is shortened with 'truncate' instead.
  //
  // This, 'viewMut' and 'truncate' are how a C function fills a list: resize
  // it to the count the function was told it may write, hand it 'viewMut()',
  // and truncate to the count the function says it wrote.
  pub fn resize(self: &mut, n: usize, value: T) where T is Copy {
    if n <= used { self.truncate(n); return; }
    self.reserve(n - used);
    while used < n {
      mem.writeRaw(ptr + used, value);
      used = used + 1usize;
    }
  }

  // A slice of every value it holds, for reading them now: a borrow of the
  // body 'Array[T]', whose methods the list folds in
  pub fn view(self: &) &Array[T] { mem.sliceFromParts(ptr, used) }

  // A cursor over its values, front to back, which 'each x in list' walks: 'next'
  // gives a borrow of each in turn until it answers None. The list may not
  // change while the cursor, or a borrow it gave, is used
  pub fn iter(self: &) ListIter[T] { ListIter[T](self, ptr, used, 0usize) }

  // A mutable slice of every value it holds, for changing them in place:
  // what a function that fills a slice writes into, a C function included.
  // The list stays borrowed mutably while the slice is used, so nothing else
  // may read or change it until the slice's last use
  pub fn viewMut(self: &mut) &mut Array[T] { mem.sliceFromPartsMut(ptr, used) }

  // A uni slice of every value it holds, for a parallel loop that changes them: a
  // list reached only by a '&uni' lends its values the same way 'viewMut' does for
  // a '&mut' one, so the slice can be the exclusive source such a loop needs
  // ('parallel each p in (&uni list).viewUni().mutItems()'). Nothing else may read
  // or change the list until the slice's last use
  pub fn viewUni(self: &uni) &uni Array[T] { mem.sliceFromPartsUni(ptr, used) }

  // The values for changing in place, which 'each x in list.mutItems()' walks:
  // 'x' is a '&mut' of each. It is a cursor over the mutable slice 'viewMut' lends
  // (so the loop is a counted one, as for any slice), and the list may not be read or
  // changed otherwise while the loop runs
  pub fn mutItems(self: &mut) ArrayMutItems[T] { ArrayMutItems[T](mem.sliceFromPartsMut(ptr, used), 0usize) }

  // How a collector reaches what the list holds, in its block: a writable
  // reference to each value, which it traces as the value is traced (and, where
  // it moves what a value refers to, rewrites in place). A list of numbers holds
  // nothing a collector need find, and this costs it nothing.
  pub fn gcTraceElements(self: &mut) ArrayMutItems[T] { ArrayMutItems[T](mem.sliceFromPartsMut(ptr, used), 0usize) }

  // The values in runs of 'n' for changing in place, each run a '&mut' slice
  // (the last may be shorter): the mutable slice's 'mutChunks'. The list may not
  // be read or changed otherwise while they are used
  pub fn mutChunks(self: &mut, n: usize, file: &Array[u8] = srcFile(), line: u32 = srcLine()) ArrayMutChunks[T] {
    mem.sliceFromPartsMut(ptr, used).mutChunks(n, file, line)
  }

  // The values as rows of 'N' for changing in place, 'list.mutArrays[3]()': the mutable
  // slice's 'mutArrays', a '&mut' slice of 'Array[T, N]' over the list's own block (its
  // 'asArrays' is the slice's, folded in). The list may not be read or changed otherwise
  // while the rows are used
  pub fn mutArrays[N: usize](self: &mut, file: &Array[u8] = srcFile(), line: u32 = srcLine()) &mut Array[Array[T, N]] {
    mem.sliceFromPartsMut(ptr, used).mutArrays[N](file, line)
  }

  // The values moved out one at a time, which 'each x in list.drain()' walks: 'x'
  // is the value itself, not a borrow of it. The list is left empty at once (its
  // block kept for reuse), and a value the loop does not reach, because it ends
  // early, is finalized with the cursor
  pub fn drain(self: &mut) ListDrain[T] {
    let n = used;
    used = 0usize;
    ListDrain[T](self, ptr, n, 0usize)
  }

  // The list is consumed: its values stay where they are, in the block it
  // owned, which becomes a Uni[Array[T]]'s. The block is shrunk to hold them,
  // never copied, and what the owner's death finalizes is each of them. A list
  // that holds nothing makes a one-byte block, since the owner of an 'Array'
  // always owns heap bytes. (A list whose region is not the heap's cannot hand
  // its block over, since the owner gives it back to the raw blocks layer: the values
  // are moved into a block of the heap's, and the old one goes back.)
  pub fn freeze(self) Uni[Array[T]] {
    var me = self;
    let n = me.used;
    let bytes = n * mem.sizeof[T]();
    let keep = if bytes == 0usize {1usize} else {bytes};
    var p = null as *T;
    if isHeap(me.region) {
      p = RawBlocks.resize(me.ptr as *u8, me.cap * mem.sizeof[T](), keep, mem.alignof[T]()) as *T;
    }
    else {
      p = RawBlocks.alloc(keep, mem.alignof[T]()) as *T;
      if n > 0usize { mem.moveRaw(p, me.ptr, n); }
      blockFree[T](me.region, me.ptr, me.cap);
    }
    me.ptr = null;
    me.used = 0usize;
    me.cap = 0usize;
    mem.sliceFromPartsUni(p, n) as Uni[Array[T]]
  }

  // THE PARALLEL BUILDER. 'List[T]() <- parallel each x in src yield v' is built
  // from these three, which the compiler calls and a program does not. Each piece
  // of the loop (a run of consecutive passes, on whichever worker) appends to a
  // list of its own, an 'emptyPiece' (the pieces never touch each other's), and
  // gives it up when it has finished, 'depositPiece', to a PieceBag; when every
  // piece has, 'joinPieces' moves the pieces' values onto the end of this list in
  // the order of the passes that made them, by where each piece began, and gives
  // the pieces' blocks back. The bag is made first, 'pieceBag'.
  pub fn pieceBag(self: &) PieceBag { PieceBag(0usize) }

  // (A piece is built on a worker of its own, so it is the heap's, not the
  // list's region, whose owner may not allow two threads in at once)
  pub fn emptyPiece(self: &) List[T] { List[T](null, 0usize, 0usize, heapRegion()) }

  // The list of the piece that began at pass 'lo', kept in the bag (a piece that
  // appended nothing is not kept). The bag's stack is pushed atomically: the
  // pieces end at the same time.
  pub fn depositPiece(self: &, bag: &mut PieceBag, lo: usize, piece: List[T]) {
    if piece.used == 0usize { return; }
    let node = libc.malloc(mem.sizeof[PieceNode[T]]()) as *PieceNode[T];
    mem.writeRaw(node, PieceNode[T](0usize, lo, piece));
    let headp = &mut bag.head as *usize;
    var seen = 0usize;
    var pushed = false;
    while !pushed {
      let head = mem.atomicLoad[usize](headp, MemOrder.Acquire);
      (*node).next = head;
      seen, pushed = mem.atomicCompareSwap[usize](headp, head, node as usize,
        MemOrder.AcqRel, MemOrder.Relaxed);
    }
  }

  // After the loop: the values of the bag's pieces, first piece first, appended to
  // this list; the bag is left empty
  pub fn joinPieces(self: &mut, bag: &mut PieceBag) {
    var pending = bag.head;
    bag.head = 0usize;
    // The stack comes in no order: insert each piece by where it began
    var sorted = 0usize;
    while pending != 0usize {
      let rec = pending as *PieceNode[T];
      pending = (*rec).next;
      if sorted == 0usize || (*rec).lo < (*(sorted as *PieceNode[T])).lo {
        (*rec).next = sorted;
        sorted = rec as usize;
      }
      else {
        var at = sorted as *PieceNode[T];
        while (*at).next != 0usize && (*((*at).next as *PieceNode[T])).lo < (*rec).lo {
          at = (*at).next as *PieceNode[T];
        }
        (*rec).next = (*at).next;
        (*at).next = rec as usize;
      }
    }
    while sorted != 0usize {
      let rec = sorted as *PieceNode[T];
      sorted = (*rec).next;
      let n = (*rec).list.used;
      if n > 0usize {
        self.reserve(n);
        mem.moveRaw(ptr + used, (*rec).list.ptr, n);
        used = used + n;
        (*rec).list.used = 0usize;
      }
      mem.finalize[List[T]](&mut (*rec).list as *List[T]);
      libc.free(rec as *u8);
    }
  }

  // Put its values in order: 'order(a, b)' is negative when a goes before b,
  // positive when after, and 0 when either may go first, as 'compare' and
  // 'bytesCompare' answer. The sort is stable: values that order calls equal
  // keep the order they had. A merge sort, n log n comparisons, through a
  // second block as big as the list's, given back before it returns.
  // 'order' is any callable of that signature that only reads what it holds,
  // met statically and so inlined into the merge: a function referenced,
  // 'names.sortBy(&byName)' with 'fn byName(a: &String, b: &String) i32', a struct
  // with that '()', or a closure written there, which may capture what it
  // compares by, an eye point say (one that changes what it borrows is refused
  // at the call).
  pub fn sortBy[F: fn(a: &T, b: &T) i32](self: &mut, order: &F) {
    if used < 2usize { return; }
    let spare = RawBlocks.alloc(used * mem.sizeof[T](), mem.alignof[T]()) as *T;
    // Each pass merges runs of 'width' values from one block into the other
    var from = ptr;
    var to = spare;
    var width = 1usize;
    while width < used {
      var lo = 0usize;
      while lo < used {
        let mid = if lo + width < used {lo + width} else {used};
        let hi = if mid + width < used {mid + width} else {used};
        var a = lo;
        var b = mid;
        var k = lo;
        // The right run's value goes first only when it is strictly before
        while a < mid && b < hi {
          if order((from + b) as &T, (from + a) as &T) < 0i32 {
            mem.moveRaw(to + k, from + b, 1usize);
            b = b + 1usize;
          }
          else {
            mem.moveRaw(to + k, from + a, 1usize);
            a = a + 1usize;
          }
          k = k + 1usize;
        }
        mem.moveRaw(to + k, from + a, mid - a);
        mem.moveRaw(to + k + (mid - a), from + b, hi - b);
        lo = hi;
      }
      let was = from;
      from = to;
      to = was;
      width = width * 2usize;
    }
    if from != ptr { mem.moveRaw(ptr, from, used); }
    RawBlocks.release(spare as *u8);
  }

  fn finalizeAll(self: &mut) {
    if mem.needsFinal[T]() {
      var i = 0usize;
      while i < used { mem.finalize(ptr + i); i = i + 1usize; }
    }
  }

  // Its death: each value it holds, first to last, then the block, given back
  // to its region
  fn final(self: &uni) {
    self.finalizeAll();
    blockFree[T](region, ptr, cap);
  }
}

// The pieces of a parallel builder, as List's 'pieceBag', 'depositPiece' and
// 'joinPieces' keep them: a stack of nodes, each a piece's list and where the
// piece began, whose top's address is 'head' (0 when there is none), pushed
// atomically as the pieces end
pub struct PieceBag {
  head: usize;
}

struct PieceNode[T] {
  next: usize;      // the node below it in the stack, as an address
  lo: usize;        // the pass the piece began at
  list: List[T];
}

// A cursor over a list's values, front to back: 'next' gives a borrow of each in
// turn. It borrows the list, which may not change while the cursor, or a value it
// gave, is used; the block and the count are read once, when it is made, so that
// a loop over it reads neither again
pub struct ListIter['a, T] is Iterator[&T] {
  list: &'a List[T];
  ptr: *T;
  end: usize;
  pos: usize;

  pub fn next(self: &mut) Option[&'a T] {
    if pos >= end { return None; }
    let i = pos;
    pos = i + 1usize;
    Some[&T]((ptr + i) as &T)
  }

  // How many values are left, the one at k of them, and the cursors over those
  // before the place 'at' of them and those from it on: a ParallelIterable and
  // RandomAccess, so that a zip of two lists can be walked by 'parallel each'
  pub fn len(self: &) usize { end - pos }

  pub fn at(self: &, k: usize) &'a T {
    if k >= end - pos {
      panic("ListIter.at: the value is past the end");
    }
    (ptr + pos + k) as &T
  }

  pub fn split(self: &, at: usize) (ListIter['a, T], ListIter['a, T]) {
    if at > end - pos {
      panic("ListIter.split: the index is past the end");
    }
    (ListIter[T](list, ptr, pos + at, pos), ListIter[T](list, ptr, end, pos + at))
  }
}

// A cursor over the values a list gave up, front to back: 'next' moves each out
// in turn. It borrows the list mutably, which may not be touched while the cursor
// is used (a push could write over a value not yet moved out). Its death
// finalizes the values not moved out.
pub struct ListDrain['a, T] is Iterator[T] {
  list: &'a mut List[T];
  ptr: *T;
  end: usize;
  pos: usize;

  pub fn next(self: &mut) Option[T] {
    if pos >= end { return None; }
    let i = pos;
    pos = i + 1usize;
    Some[T](mem.readRaw(ptr + i))
  }

  fn final(self: &uni) {
    if mem.needsFinal[T]() {
      var i = pos;
      while i < end { mem.finalize(ptr + i); i = i + 1usize; }
    }
  }
}

// -------- Deque --------

// A double-ended queue of T: a growable ring buffer (Rust's VecDeque). Its values
// sit in one block of 'cap' slots, as a circle: the first value is in slot
// 'head', the next after it, and so on round past the block's end to its start,
// 'used' of them in all. That makes a push or a pop at either end O(1), where a
// List can take a value off its front only by moving every other one down; and a
// value is reached by its position from the front, 'deque[i]', one addition and
// a compare more than a List's. Growing makes a block twice the size and moves
// the values into it in order, the front one in slot 0, which is the only time
// they move, and it never shrinks. 'push' and 'pop' work at the back, as a
// List's do; 'pushFront' and 'popFront' at the front. So a Deque is a Stack
// (at its back) and a Queue (in at the back, out at the front).
//
// Its values do not lie in one run of memory when they wrap round the block's
// end, so there is no 'view' of them: walk them with 'items', or by position.
// A borrow from a lookup or a cursor is good only until the deque next changes.
pub struct Deque[T] is ShapeChanging, Sendable, CarriesRegion, GcTraceElements {
  ptr: *T;
  head: usize;    // the slot of the first value
  used: usize;
  cap: usize;
  region: *MemRegion;

  // An empty deque, 'Deque[i32]()': no block until the first value arrives.
  // The last argument of every init is the region the block will come from
  // (see List)
  pub fn initEmpty overload init(self: &new, region: *MemRegion) { *self = Deque(null, 0usize, 0usize, 0usize, region); }

  // An empty deque whose block has room for at least n values, 'Deque[i32](100)':
  // all the room the region gives a block of n (see List)
  pub fn initCapacity overload init(self: &new, n: usize, region: *MemRegion) {
    *self = Deque(null, 0usize, 0usize, 0usize, region);
    if n > 0usize {
      let room = blockRoom[T](region, n);
      self.ptr = blockNew[T](region, n);
      self.cap = room;
    }
  }

  // An empty deque: no block until the first value arrives
  pub fn empty() Deque { Deque(null, 0usize, 0usize, 0usize, heapRegion()) }

  // An empty deque whose block already has room for n values
  pub fn withCapacity(n: usize) Deque {
    var d = Deque(null, 0usize, 0usize, 0usize, heapRegion());
    d.reserve(n);
    d
  }

  // How many values it holds, and how many its block has room for
  pub fn len(self: &) usize { used }
  pub fn capacity(self: &) usize { cap }
  pub fn isEmpty(self: &) bool { used == 0usize }

  // How a collector reaches what the deque holds, in its block: a writable
  // reference to each value, front to back (see List)
  pub fn gcTraceElements(self: &mut) DequeGcTraceItems[T] { DequeGcTraceItems[T](self, 0usize) }

  // Make room for 'more' values beyond those it holds. A block too small is
  // replaced by one twice its size, or exactly big enough if that is more, and
  // never smaller than 4; the values move into it with the first in slot 0.
  // Every borrow into the old block is then stale.
  pub fn reserve(self: &mut, more: usize) {
    if used + more <= cap { return; }
    var want = cap * 2usize;
    if want < used + more { want = used + more; }
    if want < 4usize { want = 4usize; }
    self.regrow(want);
  }

  // Move a value in at the back
  pub fn push(self: &mut, value: T) {
    self.reserve(1usize);
    mem.writeRaw(ptr + self.slot(used), value);
    used = used + 1usize;
  }

  // 'deque <- value' is 'deque.push(value)'
  pub fn append overload `<-`(self: &mut, value: T) { self.push(value); }

  // Move a value in at the front: it becomes position 0
  pub fn pushFront(self: &mut, value: T) {
    self.reserve(1usize);
    head = if head == 0usize {cap - 1usize} else {head - 1usize};
    mem.writeRaw(ptr + head, value);
    used = used + 1usize;
  }

  // Move the last value out, or None when it is empty
  pub fn pop(self: &mut) Option[T] {
    if used == 0usize { return None; }
    used = used - 1usize;
    Some[T](mem.readRaw(ptr + self.slot(used)))
  }

  // Move the first value out, or None when it is empty
  pub fn popFront(self: &mut) Option[T] {
    if used == 0usize { return None; }
    let slot = head;
    head = if head + 1usize == cap {0usize} else {head + 1usize};
    used = used - 1usize;
    Some[T](mem.readRaw(ptr + slot))
  }

  // A borrow of the last value, for reading it now, or None when it is empty
  pub fn peek(self: &) Option[&T] {
    if used == 0usize { return None; }
    Some[&T]((ptr + self.slot(used - 1usize)) as &T)
  }

  // A borrow of the first value, or None when it is empty
  pub fn peekFront(self: &) Option[&T] {
    if used == 0usize { return None; }
    Some[&T]((ptr + head) as &T)
  }

  // A borrow of the value at position index from the front, or None when index
  // is out of range
  pub fn get(self: &, index: usize) Option[&T] {
    if index >= used { return None; }
    Some[&T]((ptr + self.slot(index)) as &T)
  }

  // A borrow of the value at position index from the front, for reading it
  // now. An index out of range is a panic at the line of the caller
  pub fn `[]`(self: &, index: usize, file: &Array[u8] = srcFile(), line: u32 = srcLine()) &T {
    if index >= used { outOfRange(index, used, file, line); }
    (ptr + self.slot(index)) as &T
  }

  // A mutable borrow of the value at position index, for changing it in place:
  // '&mut deque[i]'
  pub fn `&[]`(self: &mut, index: usize, file: &Array[u8] = srcFile(), line: u32 = srcLine()) &mut T {
    if index >= used { outOfRange(index, used, file, line); }
    (ptr + self.slot(index)) as &mut T
  }

  // Finalize every value, front to back, and keep the block for reuse
  pub fn clear(self: &mut) {
    self.finalizeAll();
    head = 0usize;
    used = 0usize;
  }

  // A cursor over its values, front to back: 'next' gives a borrow of each in
  // turn until it answers None. The deque may not change while it is used.
  pub fn items(self: &) DequeItems[T] { DequeItems[T](self, 0usize) }

  // The same cursor under the name 'each x in deque' walks it by
  pub fn iter(self: &) DequeItems[T] { DequeItems[T](self, 0usize) }

  // The cursor 'each x in &mut deque' walks by: the same
  // values, front to back, each lent to be changed in place
  pub fn viewMut(self: &mut) DequeMutItems[T] { DequeMutItems[T](self, 0usize) }

  // The slot of the value at position i, which may be the one just past the last
  // when the caller is about to fill it
  fn slot(self: &, i: usize) usize {
    let s = head + i;
    if s >= cap {s - cap} else {s}
  }

  // A new block of 'want' slots (more than 'used') with every value moved into
  // it in order, the front one in slot 0. The old block holds up to two runs:
  // from 'head' to the block's end, and from its start round to the last value
  fn regrow(self: &mut, want: usize) {
    let room = blockRoom[T](region, want);
    let fresh = blockNew[T](region, want);
    if used > 0usize {
      let firstRun = if head + used <= cap {used} else {cap - head};
      mem.moveRaw(fresh, ptr + head, firstRun);
      mem.moveRaw(fresh + firstRun, ptr, used - firstRun);
    }
    blockFree[T](region, ptr, cap);
    ptr = fresh;
    head = 0usize;
    cap = room;
  }

  fn finalizeAll(self: &mut) {
    if mem.needsFinal[T]() {
      var i = 0usize;
      while i < used {
        mem.finalize(ptr + self.slot(i));
        i = i + 1usize;
      }
    }
  }

  // Its death: each value it holds, front to back, then the block, given back
  // to its region
  fn final(self: &uni) {
    self.finalizeAll();
    blockFree[T](region, ptr, cap);
  }
}

// A cursor over a deque's values, front to back: 'next' gives a borrow of each
// in turn. It borrows the deque, which may not change while the cursor, or a
// value it gave, is used
pub struct DequeItems['a, T] is Iterator[&T] {
  deque: &'a Deque[T];
  pos: usize;

  pub fn next(self: &mut) Option[&'a T] {
    if pos >= deque.used { return None; }
    let i = pos;
    pos = i + 1usize;
    Some[&T]((deque.ptr + deque.slot(i)) as &T)
  }
}

// A cursor over a deque's values, front to back: 'next' gives a '&mut' borrow of
// each in turn, to change it in place. It borrows the deque mutably, which may
// not change while the cursor, or a value it gave, is used
pub struct DequeMutItems['a, T] is Iterator[&mut T] {
  deque: &'a mut Deque[T];
  pos: usize;

  pub fn next(self: &mut) Option[&'a mut T] {
    if pos >= deque.used { return None; }
    let i = pos;
    pos = i + 1usize;
    Some[&mut T]((deque.ptr + deque.slot(i)) as &mut T)
  }
}

// A cursor over a deque's values, front to back, for a collector to trace
// them: 'next' gives a writable reference to each in turn. It borrows the deque
// mutably, which may not change while the cursor is used
pub struct DequeGcTraceItems['a, T] is Iterator[&mut T] {
  deque: &'a mut Deque[T];
  pos: usize;

  pub fn next(self: &mut) Option[&'a mut T] {
    if pos >= deque.used { return None; }
    let i = pos;
    pos = i + 1usize;
    Some[&mut T]((deque.ptr + deque.slot(i)) as &mut T)
  }
}

// -------- String --------

// An owned, growable string: text, UTF-8 by convention, though the methods
// that change it look at no more than bytes. It is a List[u8] and one spare
// byte, so it lives, moves and dies as a list does. Its block always has a NUL
// after the bytes (which the length leaves out), rewritten by each change, so
// that C can be handed its bytes with no copy ('cstr'); a String that holds
// nothing has no block at all.
//
// It lends a '&str', its 'view()', and folds the methods of 'str' in as its
// own (the 'use' line below): 's.trimStart()', 's.isCharBoundary(i)', 's <
// t', each is written once on 'str'. A literal is a '&str', and wherever a
// String is wanted a literal is copied into one ('fromLiteral'), so
// 'imm s String = "core"' and 'names.push("core")'; where a '&String' is wanted
// the copy is a temporary, lent and dropped at the statement's end. The methods below that
// take text take a '&str'; a '&Array[u8]' is bytes, and meets a String through
// the 'Bytes' forms ('fromBytes', 'appendBytes'), which look at no more than the
// bytes. The methods of 'str' that a String has no reason to differ from, 'find'
// and 'startsWith' among them, are the fold's.
pub struct String is ShapeChanging, Hash, CarriesRegion {
  buf: List[u8];

  use str via view but len, isEmpty, cstr, `[]`, trim, eq, `==`, hash;

  // An empty String, 'String()': no block until the first byte arrives. The
  // last argument of every init is the region the bytes will sit in (see List),
  // which the String hands its List
  pub fn initEmpty overload init(self: &new, region: *MemRegion) {
    *self = String(List[u8](region));
  }

  // An empty String with room for n bytes (and the NUL after them), 'String(100)'
  pub fn initCapacity overload init(self: &new, n: usize, region: *MemRegion) {
    if n == 0usize { *self = String(List[u8](region)); }
    else { *self = String(List[u8](n + 1usize, region)); }
  }

  pub fn empty() String { String(List[u8].empty()) }

  // An empty String with room for n bytes (and the NUL after them)
  pub fn withCapacity(n: usize) String {
    if n == 0usize { return String.empty(); }
    String(List[u8].withCapacity(n + 1usize))
  }

  // A String holding a copy of this text
  pub fn from(s: &str) String {
    var str = String.withCapacity(s.len());
    str.append(s);
    str
  }

  // A String holding a copy of these bytes, for bytes that are text (a buffer
  // read from a file or a socket, a part of one). Nothing checks they are
  // UTF-8: strings are UTF-8 by convention, as 'bytes as &str' is.
  pub fn fromBytes(b: &Array[u8]) String {
    var str = String.withCapacity(b.len);
    str.appendBytes(b);
    str
  }

  // A String holding a copy of this text: what a literal becomes where a
  // String is wanted, '"core"' in the place of 'String.from("core")'
  pub fn fromLiteral(s: &str) String { String.from(s) }

  // The bytes it holds, for the methods here that work on bytes, and the NUL
  // kept after them. The block is nothing until the first byte arrives.
  fn raw(self: &) &Array[u8] inline { mem.sliceFromParts(buf.ptr, buf.used) }

  // Write the NUL after the bytes, making room for it first. A String with no
  // block has nothing to terminate: its view is the static empty literal.
  fn seal(self: &mut) {
    if buf.ptr == null { return; }
    if buf.used >= buf.cap { buf.reserve(1usize); }
    mem.writeRaw(buf.ptr + buf.used, 0u8);
  }

  // A String holding a copy of the bytes of a C string, up to its NUL: what
  // a C function answers, or one of main's 'argv'. It takes a 'cstr', which
  // promises the zero that ends it and is never null. A raw pointer does not
  // promise that (and a '&u8' converts to one), so none is taken: where C may
  // leave one null, the caller asks first, and 'cstr.fromPtr(p)' vouches for
  // the rest.
  pub fn fromCStr(c: CStr) String { String.from(c.view()) }

  // The views of 'parts' one after another, with 'sep' between each two:
  // 'String.join(names.view(), ", ")'. No parts make an empty String.
  pub fn join(parts: &Array[String], sep: &str) String {
    var out = String.empty();
    var i = 0usize;
    while i < parts.len {
      if i > 0usize { out.append(sep); }
      out.append(parts[i].view());
      i = i + 1usize;
    }
    out
  }

  // A copy of this one. (Not 'clone': a method of that name is the lifecycle
  // method that makes a type's values copyable, which a String's are not.)
  pub fn copy(self: &) String { String.from(self.view()) }

  pub fn len(self: &) usize { buf.used }
  pub fn isEmpty(self: &) bool { buf.used == 0usize }

  // Its text, for reading it now: the static empty literal when it holds
  // nothing, else its bytes. While the borrow is used the String may not change.
  pub fn view(self: &) &str {
    if buf.used == 0usize { return ""; }
    self.raw() as &str
  }

  // Its bytes, for changing them in place (nothing checks they stay UTF-8)
  pub fn viewMut(self: &mut) &mut Array[u8] { buf.viewMut() }

  // Its bytes as a uni slice, when the String is reached only by a '&uni': the
  // list's 'viewUni', for a parallel loop that changes them
  pub fn viewUni(self: &uni) &uni Array[u8] { (&uni buf).viewUni() }

  // The String is consumed: its bytes stay where they are, in the block it
  // owned, which becomes a Uni[str]'s. The block is shrunk to hold the text and
  // its NUL, never copied. A String that holds nothing makes a one-byte block,
  // since the owner of a 'str' always owns heap bytes. (A String whose region is
  // not the heap's moves its bytes into a heap block, as a List's freeze does.)
  pub fn freeze(self) Uni[str] {
    var me = self;
    let n = me.buf.used;
    var p = null as *u8;
    if isHeap(me.buf.region) {
      p = RawBlocks.resize(me.buf.ptr as *u8, me.buf.cap, n + 1usize, 1usize);
    }
    else {
      p = RawBlocks.alloc(n + 1usize, 1usize);
      if n > 0usize { mem.moveRaw(p, me.buf.ptr, n); }
      blockFree[u8](me.buf.region, me.buf.ptr, me.buf.cap);
    }
    p[n] = 0u8;
    me.buf.ptr = null;
    me.buf.used = 0usize;
    me.buf.cap = 0usize;
    mem.sliceFromParts(p, n) as Uni[str]
  }

  // The byte at index
  pub fn `[]`(self: &, index: usize) u8 { *buf[index] }

  // -------- adding to the end --------

  // Add the text s at the end. Rust's 'push_str'.
  pub fn append overload `<-`(self: &mut, s: &str) {
    self.appendBytes(s);
  }

  // Add these bytes at the end, for bytes that are text; nothing checks they
  // are UTF-8 (see 'fromBytes')
  pub fn appendBytes(self: &mut, b: &Array[u8]) {
    buf.reserve(b.len + 1usize);
    var i = 0usize;
    while i < b.len { buf.push(b[i]); i = i + 1usize; }
    self.seal();
  }

  pub fn appendByte(self: &mut, b: u8) {
    buf.push(b);
    self.seal();
  }

  // An integer in decimal, with a '-' before a negative one
  pub fn appendInt overload `<-`(self: &mut, n: i64) {
    if n < 0i64 {
      buf.push('-');
      // The magnitude as a u64, which holds even the most negative i64's
      self.appendUInt(0u64 - u64.from(n));
    }
    else {
      self.appendUInt(u64.from(n));
    }
  }

  pub fn appendUInt overload `<-`(self: &mut, n: u64) {
    var digits = Array[u8, 20]() <- fill 0u8;
    var count = 0usize;
    var rest = n;
    var going = true;
    while going {
      digits[count] = u8.from(rest % 10u64) + '0';
      count = count + 1usize;
      rest = rest / 10u64;
      going = rest > 0u64;
    }
    while count > 0usize {
      count = count - 1usize;
      buf.push(digits[count]);
    }
    self.seal();
  }

  // A float as the shortest text that reads back as it (core's FloatText):
  // "0.1", "1.0", "-0.0", "1e+16", "inf". An f32 has its own, 'appendF32':
  // given to '<-' it is widened to an f64 and written as one
  pub fn appendF64 overload `<-`(self: &mut, x: f64) {
    self.append(FloatText(x).text());
  }

  // An f32 as the shortest text that reads back as that f32: 0.1f32 is "0.1"
  pub fn appendF32(self: &mut, x: f32) {
    self.append(FloatText.ofF32(x).text());
  }

  // The low 'width' hex digits of n, lowercase, leading zeros kept
  pub fn appendHex(self: &mut, n: u64, width: usize) {
    var d = width;
    while d > 0usize {
      d = d - 1usize;
      let nibble = u8.from((n >> u64.from(d * 4usize)) & 15u64);
      buf.push(if nibble < 10u8 {'0' + nibble} else {'a' + nibble - 10u8});
    }
    self.seal();
  }

  // Put s into the text so that it begins at byte index: "ac" with "b" put at
  // 1 is "abc". The index is a position between characters, 0 up to the
  // length, and anything else is a program error.
  pub fn insert(self: &mut, index: usize, s: &str, file: &Array[u8] = srcFile(), line: u32 = srcLine()) {
    if index > buf.used || !self.view().isCharBoundary(index) {
      panic("collections: String.insert at a position that is not between characters", file, line);
    }
    let text: &Array[u8] = s;
    if text.len == 0usize { return; }
    let old = buf.used;
    buf.reserve(text.len + 1usize);
    mem.moveRaw(buf.ptr + index + text.len, buf.ptr + index, old - index);
    var i = 0usize;
    while i < text.len { mem.writeRaw(buf.ptr + index + i, text[i]); i = i + 1usize; }
    buf.used = old + text.len;
    self.seal();
  }

  // Take away the bytes from start up to, not including, end: "abcd" with 1
  // to 3 removed is "ad". Both are positions between characters, start not
  // after end and end not past the length, or it is a program error.
  pub fn remove(self: &mut, start: usize, end: usize, file: &Array[u8] = srcFile(), line: u32 = srcLine()) {
    let v = self.view();
    if start > end || end > buf.used || !(v.isCharBoundary(start) && v.isCharBoundary(end)) {
      panic("collections: String.remove of a range that is not between characters", file, line);
    }
    mem.moveRaw(buf.ptr + start, buf.ptr + end, buf.used - end);
    buf.used = buf.used - (end - start);
    self.seal();
  }

  // Cut it to n bytes, keeping its block; one of n bytes or fewer is left
  // alone. n is a position between characters, or it is a program error.
  pub fn truncate(self: &mut, n: usize, file: &Array[u8] = srcFile(), line: u32 = srcLine()) {
    if n >= buf.used { return; }
    if !self.view().isCharBoundary(n) {
      panic("collections: String.truncate to a length that is not between characters", file, line);
    }
    buf.used = n;
    self.seal();
  }

  // Every '\' made a '/', or any byte made another
  pub fn replaceByte(self: &mut, from: u8, to: u8) {
    var i = 0usize;
    while i < buf.used {
      if *buf[i] == from { buf.set(i, to); }
      i = i + 1usize;
    }
  }

  // Every 'from' made 'to', left to right, the pieces never overlapping:
  // "aaa" with "aa" made "b" is "ba". An empty 'from' is found before each
  // byte and at the end, so "ab" with "" made "-" is "-a-b-".
  pub fn replace(self: &mut, from: &str, to: &str) {
    let v = self.raw();
    let f: &Array[u8] = from;
    var out = String(v.len, buf.region);
    var i = 0usize;
    if f.len == 0usize {
      while i < v.len { out.append(to); out.appendByte(v[i]); i = i + 1usize; }
      out.append(to);
    }
    else {
      while i < v.len {
        if i + f.len <= v.len && &v[i..<i + f.len] == f {
          out.append(to);
          i = i + f.len;
        }
        else {
          out.appendByte(v[i]);
          i = i + 1usize;
        }
      }
    }
    *self = out;
  }

  // 'fill' bytes, spaces unless another is given, put before its bytes until
  // it is 'width' bytes long: "7" padded to 3 is "  7", which right-aligns
  // it. One that long already, or longer, is left as it is. Width counts
  // bytes, so a character beyond ASCII counts each of its UTF-8 bytes.
  pub fn padStart(self: &mut, width: usize, fill: u8 = ' ') {
    if buf.used >= width { return; }
    var out = String(width, buf.region);
    var n = width - buf.used;
    while n > 0usize { out.appendByte(fill); n = n - 1usize; }
    out.appendBytes(self.raw());
    *self = out;
  }

  // The same, put after its bytes: "ab" padded to 4 is "ab  ", which
  // left-aligns it
  pub fn padEnd(self: &mut, width: usize, fill: u8 = ' ') {
    while buf.used < width { buf.push(fill); }
    self.seal();
  }

  // Each ASCII capital letter, A to Z, made small; every other byte, those
  // of characters beyond ASCII included, left as it is
  pub fn lowerAscii(self: &mut) {
    var i = 0usize;
    while i < buf.used {
      let b = *buf[i];
      buf.set(i, asciiLower(b));
      i = i + 1usize;
    }
  }

  // Each ASCII small letter, a to z, made a capital; every other byte left
  pub fn upperAscii(self: &mut) {
    var i = 0usize;
    while i < buf.used {
      let b = *buf[i];
      buf.set(i, asciiUpper(b));
      i = i + 1usize;
    }
  }

  // Empty it, keeping its block
  pub fn clear(self: &mut) {
    buf.clear();
    self.seal();
  }

  // -------- reading --------

  pub fn equals(self: &, s: &str) bool { self.view() == s }

  // Whether it holds the same bytes as s once ASCII letters are made one
  // case: "Path" equals "PATH" this way. Bytes beyond ASCII must match exactly.
  pub fn equalsIgnoreAsciiCase(self: &, text: &str) bool {
    let v = self.raw();
    let s: &Array[u8] = text;
    if v.len != s.len { return false; }
    var i = 0usize;
    while i < v.len {
      if asciiLower(v[i]) != asciiLower(s[i]) { return false; }
      i = i + 1usize;
    }
    true
  }

  // Whether two hold the same bytes: the '==' that Hash requires of a type
  // beside its 'hash'
  pub fn eq overload `==`(self: &, o: &String) bool inline { self.view() == o.view() }

  // What identifies it to a Hasher (core's Hash): what its text feeds (str's
  // 'hash'), so a literal, a view and a String of the same bytes hash alike
  pub fn hash(self: &, h: &mut Hasher) inline { self.view().hash(h); }

  // -------- copies of parts --------

  // A copy of the bytes from 'start' up to, not including, 'end'
  pub fn sub(self: &, start: usize, end: usize) String {
    let v = self.raw();
    String.fromBytes(&v[start..<end])
  }

  // A copy without the spaces, tabs, carriage returns and line feeds at
  // either end
  pub fn trim(self: &) String {
    let v = self.raw();
    var a = 0usize;
    var b = v.len;
    while a < b && isSpace(v[a]) { a = a + 1usize; }
    while b > a && isSpace(v[b - 1usize]) { b = b - 1usize; }
    String.fromBytes(&v[a..<b])
  }

  // Copies of its lines. A line ends at '\n', and a '\r' just before it is
  // dropped; a last line need not end in '\n', and an ending '\n' does not
  // begin another line, so "a\nb\n" and "a\r\nb" are both two lines, and ""
  // is none.
  pub fn lines(self: &) List[String] {
    let v = self.raw();
    var out = List[String].empty();
    var start = 0usize;
    var i = 0usize;
    while i < v.len {
      if v[i] == '\n' {
        var end = i;
        if end > start && v[end - 1usize] == '\r' { end = end - 1usize; }
        out.push(String.fromBytes(&v[start..<end]));
        start = i + 1usize;
      }
      i = i + 1usize;
    }
    if start < v.len {
      var end = v.len;
      if v[end - 1usize] == '\r' { end = end - 1usize; }
      out.push(String.fromBytes(&v[start..<end]));
    }
    out
  }

  // Copies of the pieces between each 'sep': "a,b,,c" is four pieces, the
  // third empty; "" is one empty piece
  pub fn split(self: &, sep: u8) List[String] {
    let v = self.raw();
    var out = List[String].empty();
    var start = 0usize;
    var i = 0usize;
    while i < v.len {
      if v[i] == sep {
        out.push(String.fromBytes(&v[start..<i]));
        start = i + 1usize;
      }
      i = i + 1usize;
    }
    out.push(String.fromBytes(&v[start..]));
    out
  }

  // Its bytes as a C string, for a C function: they already have a NUL after
  // them, so nothing is copied. It is good until the string next changes or
  // dies; the NUL is not one of its bytes. A NUL inside the text would end the
  // string early for C, so it is a panic, at the line of the caller. An empty
  // String holds no block, and its C string is the static empty literal.
  pub fn cstr(self: &, file: &Array[u8] = srcFile(), line: u32 = srcLine()) CStr {
    if buf.ptr == null { return ""; }
    if libc.memchr(buf.ptr, 0i32, buf.used) != null {
      panic("collections: String.cstr of text holding a NUL byte, which would end the C string early", file, line);
    }
    CStr.fromPtr(buf.ptr)
  }
}

// -------- Dict --------

// A dictionary from keys to values: a SwissTable, the open-addressed hash table
// of Google's Abseil, which Rust's HashMap (hashbrown) took. Its buckets are a
// power of two, and beside them is one control byte for each: empty (0xFF),
// removed (0x80, a key was there, so a search must go on past it), or full, the
// low 7 bits of which are the top 7 bits of the key's hash (its tag). A search
// reads the control bytes eight at a time, as one 64-bit word, and tells at once
// which of the eight have the tag it wants, and which are empty, with a few
// bitwise operations (hashbrown's portable form, with no SIMD: a SIMD type
// would test 16 at a time, and is the next step when the language has one).
// Only a bucket whose tag matches has its key compared, so a miss almost never
// touches an entry. The groups are probed in a triangular sequence, so every
// bucket is reached, and the table holds at most 7/8 of its buckets (full ones
// and removed ones together, so an empty bucket always ends a search), growing
// to double when it passes that.
//
// K is any type that is Hash (core's trait, which also requires its '=='), so
// ids, integers, structs and Strings all work: 'Dict[pool.Ref[T], V]',
// 'Dict[u64, V]', 'Dict[String, V]', 'Dict[Uni[str], V]', a struct declared
// 'is Hash'. A lookup takes a '&K'. (A Dict of String, Uni[str] or Rc[str] keys
// also takes a key as a '&str', the body they lend or hold, so a literal, a view
// or any owner of text finds a key without building one.)
//
// Each dictionary keeps a hasher of its own, started from a seed, and hashes a
// key by feeding it to a copy of that hasher. 'empty' takes the process's seed
// (core's 'Hasher.seeded'), random on every run, so the order of 'keys',
// 'values' and 'entries' is different on every run: sort them, or look a key
// up, and never rely on the order. 'deterministic' fixes the seed, and then the
// same keys inserted in the same order are visited in the same order on every
// run and build. Speed is the same either way.
//
// Entries are in one block with their control bytes after them, the block
// starting at 8 buckets (room for 7 entries) when the first entry arrives.
// Nothing here is safe to change from two threads at once, and a borrow from
// a lookup, a 'keys' cursor and the like is good only until the dictionary next
// changes: an insert may move every entry.

// One bucket's key and value: live only where its control byte says full
pub struct DictEntry[K, V] {
  pub key: K;
  pub value: V;
}

const ctrlEmpty = 0xFFu8;
const ctrlRemoved = 0x80u8;
const lowBytes = 0x0101010101010101u64;
const highBits = 0x8080808080808080u64;
// The fixed seed of 'deterministic': the first digits of pi
const fixedSeed = 0x243F6A8885A308D3u64;
// What a search answers when the key is not there
const noBucket = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFusize;

// The 8 control bytes from pos on, as one word (the first byte is the lowest),
// read however pos is aligned. The last 8 control bytes copy the first 8, so a
// group that begins near the end wraps around
fn ctrlGroup(ctrl: *u8, pos: usize) u64 { mem.readRaw[u64]((ctrl + pos) as *u64) }

// Set bucket i's control byte, and its copy past the end if it is among the first 8
fn ctrlSet(ctrl: *u8, mask: usize, i: usize, v: u8) {
  mem.writeRaw[u8](ctrl + i, v);
  mem.writeRaw[u8](ctrl + (((i + mask + 1usize - 8usize) & mask) + 8usize), v);
}

// Make bucket i's control byte empty if no search can have gone past it (a
// full group around it that has an empty bucket on one side), else removed.
// It answers whether it became empty
fn ctrlErase(ctrl: *u8, mask: usize, i: usize) bool {
  let before = matchEmpty(ctrlGroup(ctrl, (i + mask + 1usize - 8usize) & mask));
  let after = matchEmpty(ctrlGroup(ctrl, i));
  if (before.leadingZeros() >> 3u32) + (after.trailingZeros() >> 3u32) >= 8u32 {
    ctrlSet(ctrl, mask, i, ctrlRemoved);
    return false;
  }
  ctrlSet(ctrl, mask, i, ctrlEmpty);
  true
}

// A flag (0x80) in each byte of g that holds t, and perhaps one that doesn't, in
// a byte just after one that does
fn matchTag(g: u64, t: u8) u64 {
  let x = g ^ (lowBytes * u64.from(t));
  (x - lowBytes) & ~x & highBits
}

// A flag in each byte of g that is empty
fn matchEmpty(g: u64) u64 { g & (g << 1u64) & highBits }

// A flag in each byte of g that is empty or removed
fn matchFree(g: u64) u64 { g & highBits }

// Which byte (0 to 7) the lowest flag in m is in
fn flagByte(m: u64) usize { usize.from(m.trailingZeros() >> 3u32) }

// The tag of a hash: its top 7 bits
fn hashTag(hash: u64) u8 { u8.from(hash >> 57u64) }

// The first empty or removed bucket in the probe sequence of hash. The table
// always has an empty bucket, so the search ends
fn freeBucket(ctrl: *u8, mask: usize, hash: u64) usize {
  var pos = usize.from(hash) & mask;
  var stride = 0usize;
  while true {
    let m = matchFree(ctrlGroup(ctrl, pos));
    if m != 0u64 { return (pos + flagByte(m)) & mask; }
    stride = stride + 8usize;
    pos = (pos + stride) & mask;
  }
  pos
}

// How many entries a table of this many buckets (a power of two, 8 or more)
// holds, 7 in every 8 buckets
fn bucketsCapacity(buckets: usize) usize { buckets / 8usize * 7usize }

// The fewest buckets (a power of two, 8 or more) that hold n entries
fn capacityBuckets(n: usize) usize {
  var b = 8usize;
  while bucketsCapacity(b) < n { b = b * 2usize; }
  b
}

pub struct Dict[K: Hash, V] is ShapeChanging, Sendable, CarriesRegion, GcTraceElements {
  ctrl: *u8;
  slots: *DictEntry[K, V];
  mask: usize;       // the buckets, less one
  items: usize;      // how many hold entries
  left: usize;       // how many more empty buckets may be filled before it grows
  hasher: Hasher;    // starts every key's hash: the seed, mixed
  region: *MemRegion;   // where the block comes from and goes back to

  // An empty dictionary, 'Dict[String, i64]()': no block until the first entry
  // arrives. Its seed is the process's, so its order differs from run to run.
  // The last argument of every init is the region the block will come from (see
  // List), which a construction that names none leaves out and is handed the heap's.
  pub fn initEmpty overload init(self: &new, region: *MemRegion) {
    *self = Dict(null, null, 0usize, 0usize, 0usize, Hasher.seeded(), region);
  }

  // An empty dictionary whose block already has room for n entries without
  // growing (rounded up to the table's size, so 'capacity' may be more),
  // 'Dict[String, i64](100)'
  pub fn initCapacity overload init(self: &new, n: usize, region: *MemRegion) {
    var d = Dict(null, null, 0usize, 0usize, 0usize, Hasher.seeded(), region);
    if n > 0usize { d.resize(capacityBuckets(n)); }
    *self = d;
  }

  pub fn empty() Dict { Dict(null, null, 0usize, 0usize, 0usize, Hasher.seeded(), heapRegion()) }

  // An empty dictionary whose block already has room for n entries without growing
  pub fn withCapacity(n: usize) Dict {
    var d = Dict(null, null, 0usize, 0usize, 0usize, Hasher.seeded(), heapRegion());
    if n > 0usize { d.resize(capacityBuckets(n)); }
    d
  }

  // An empty dictionary with a fixed seed: the same keys inserted in the same
  // order are visited in the same order on every run
  pub fn deterministic() Dict { Dict.withSeed(fixedSeed) }

  // An empty dictionary with this seed: a key hashes to what 'Hasher.withSeed(seed)'
  // makes of what it feeds, and another seed shuffles the order
  pub fn withSeed(seed: u64) Dict { Dict(null, null, 0usize, 0usize, 0usize, Hasher.withSeed(seed), heapRegion()) }

  // How many entries it holds, and how many it holds before it next grows
  pub fn len(self: &) usize { items }
  pub fn capacity(self: &) usize { items + left }
  pub fn isEmpty(self: &) bool { items == 0usize }

  // Move key and value in. A key it already holds has its entry replaced: the
  // old key is finalized and the old value moved out to the caller, as an
  // Option (Rust's HashMap::insert); None when the key was new. The Option's
  // value dies with the caller's binding, or at the end of a statement that
  // ignores it.
  pub fn insert(self: &mut, key: K, value: V) Option[V] {
    let hash = self.hashOf(&key);
    let found = self.freeOrFound(hash, &key);
    if self.isFull(found) {
      let slot = (slots + found) as &mut DictEntry[K, V];
      mem.finalize(&slot.key as *K);
      let old = mem.readRaw(&slot.value as *V);
      mem.writeRaw(slots + found, DictEntry[K, V](key, value));
      return Some[V](old);
    }
    self.fill(self.roomFor(hash, found), hash, key, value);
    None
  }

  // 'dict <- key: value' is 'dict.insert(key, value)': a later value for a key
  // it holds replaces the earlier
  pub fn insertPair overload `<-`(self: &mut, key: K, value: V) { self.insert(key, value); }

  // A borrow of key's value for changing it in place, which is how a count is
  // kept: the value already there, or else the one given, moved in, with
  // one search. A key and a value that were not needed die.
  //
  //   *counts.getOrInsert(word, 0) += 1;
  pub fn getOrInsert(self: &mut, key: K, value: V) &mut V {
    let hash = self.hashOf(&key);
    let found = self.freeOrFound(hash, &key);
    if self.isFull(found) { return &mut slots[found].value; }
    let i = self.roomFor(hash, found);
    self.fill(i, hash, key, value);
    &mut slots[i].value
  }

  // The lookups. Each takes a '&K', the key itself (which is all an id key
  // has), and, for a key that is a String, a Uni[str] or an Rc[str], a '&str',
  // the body it lends or holds: a literal, a view of some text, a Uni[str] and an
  // Rc[str] are all lent as one, and find the key they equal with no String
  // built. (An owner of text is Hash, feeding what 'str' feeds. Inserting a
  // literal into a Dict of Uni[str] copies it into the owner.) They hash and compare as the key
  // does ('str' feeds a Hasher what a String does, and '==' compares the
  // bytes), so whatever equals a key finds its bucket. An overload set names
  // every member, so the ones for text end in Text; callers write the plain
  // name.

  // Whether it holds key
  pub fn containsKey overload contains(self: &, key: &K) bool { self.find(self.hashOf(key), key) != noBucket }
  pub fn containsText overload contains(self: &, key: &str) bool where K is String || K is Uni[str] || K is Rc[str] {
    self.find(self.hashOf(key), key) != noBucket
  }

  // A borrow of key's value, for reading it now, or None
  pub fn getKey overload get(self: &, key: &K) Option[&V] {
    let i = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if i == noBucket { return None; }
    Some[&V](&slots[i].value)
  }
  pub fn getText overload get(self: &, key: &str) Option[&V] where K is String || K is Uni[str] || K is Rc[str] {
    let i = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if i == noBucket { return None; }
    Some[&V](&slots[i].value)
  }

  // A borrow of key's value for changing it in place, or None
  pub fn getMutKey overload getMut(self: &mut, key: &K) Option[&mut V] {
    let i = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if i == noBucket { return None; }
    Some[&mut V](&mut slots[i].value)
  }
  pub fn getMutText overload getMut(self: &mut, key: &str) Option[&mut V] where K is String || K is Uni[str] || K is Rc[str] {
    let i = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if i == noBucket { return None; }
    Some[&mut V](&mut slots[i].value)
  }

  // A copy of key's value, or None. This is the lookup that answers without a
  // borrow into the table, so it works through a shared owner, and it exists
  // only where V copies: a number, an id, a reference-counted owner
  pub fn getCopyKey overload getCopy(self: &, key: &K) Option[V] where V is Copy {
    let i = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if i == noBucket { return None; }
    Some[V](slots[i].value)
  }
  pub fn getCopyText overload getCopy(self: &, key: &str) Option[V] where (K is String || K is Uni[str] || K is Rc[str]) && V is Copy {
    let i = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if i == noBucket { return None; }
    Some[V](slots[i].value)
  }

  // 'dict[&key]': a borrow of key's value. A key it does not hold is a program
  // error, a panic at the line of the caller (naming the key, when it is
  // given as text): ask 'contains' first.
  pub fn indexKey overload `[]`(self: &, key: &K, file: &Array[u8] = srcFile(), line: u32 = srcLine()) &V {
    let i = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if i == noBucket { panic("collections: Dict has no entry for the key", file, line); }
    &slots[i].value
  }
  pub fn indexText overload `[]`(self: &, key: &str, file: &Array[u8] = srcFile(), line: u32 = srcLine()) &V where K is String || K is Uni[str] || K is Rc[str] {
    let i = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if i == noBucket { missingKey(key, file, line); }
    &slots[i].value
  }

  // '&mut dict[&key]': a borrow of key's value for changing it, a panic as above
  pub fn indexMutKey overload `&[]`(self: &mut, key: &K, file: &Array[u8] = srcFile(), line: u32 = srcLine()) &mut V {
    let i = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if i == noBucket { panic("collections: Dict has no entry for the key", file, line); }
    &mut slots[i].value
  }
  pub fn indexMutText overload `&[]`(self: &mut, key: &str, file: &Array[u8] = srcFile(), line: u32 = srcLine()) &mut V where K is String || K is Uni[str] || K is Rc[str] {
    let i = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if i == noBucket { missingKey(key, file, line); }
    &mut slots[i].value
  }

  // Move key's value out and finalize the key it was held under, or None when it
  // holds no such key
  pub fn removeKey overload remove(self: &mut, key: &K) Option[V] {
    let i = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if i == noBucket { return None; }
    self.take(i)
  }
  pub fn removeText overload remove(self: &mut, key: &str) Option[V] where K is String || K is Uni[str] || K is Rc[str] {
    let i = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if i == noBucket { return None; }
    self.take(i)
  }

  // Finalize every key and value, first bucket to last, and keep the block:
  // it holds nothing, with the room it had
  pub fn clear(self: &mut) {
    if ctrl == null { return; }
    if mem.needsFinal[DictEntry[K, V]]() {
      var i = 0usize;
      while i <= mask {
        if self.isFull(i) { mem.finalize(slots + i); }
        i = i + 1usize;
      }
    }
    libc.memset(ctrl, 255i32, mask + 1usize + 8usize);
    items = 0usize;
    left = bucketsCapacity(mask + 1usize);
  }

  // Cursors over what it holds, each giving a borrow into the table for reading
  // now, with 'next' until it answers None, in the table's order: different on
  // every run unless it was made with 'deterministic' or 'withSeed'. The table
  // may not change while one is used.
  pub fn keys(self: &) DictKeys[K, V] { DictKeys[K, V](self, 0usize) }
  pub fn values(self: &) DictValues[K, V] { DictValues[K, V](self, 0usize) }
  pub fn entries(self: &) DictEntries[K, V] { DictEntries[K, V](self, 0usize) }

  // How a collector reaches what the dictionary holds, in its block: a
  // writable reference to each value (see List); the keys are not walked
  pub fn gcTraceElements(self: &mut) DictGcTraceItems[K, V] { DictGcTraceItems[K, V](self, 0usize) }

  // A cursor over its pairs, which 'each k, v in dict' walks: 'next' gives a
  // pair of borrows, a key and its value, in the table's order
  pub fn iter(self: &) DictPairs[K, V] { DictPairs[K, V](self, 0usize) }

  // The cursor 'each k, v in &mut dict' walks by: the
  // key to read and its value to change in place.
  // A key is never lent to be written: it is hashed where it sits.
  pub fn viewMut(self: &mut) DictMutPairs[K, V] { DictMutPairs[K, V](self, 0usize) }

  // The hash of a key: the key fed to this dictionary's hasher. '(*key)' and
  // not 'key': a Uni[str] or Rc[str] key is a reference itself, and a method
  // call reads through one reference only, so it is read through to its text
  fn hashOf[B](self: &, key: &B) u64 {
    var h = hasher;
    (*key).hash(&mut h);
    h.finish()
  }

  // Whether the bucket holds an entry (its control byte's top bit is clear)
  fn isFull(self: &, i: usize) bool { mem.readRaw[u8](ctrl + i) < ctrlRemoved }

  // The bucket holding key, or noBucket
  fn find[B](self: &, hash: u64, key: &B) usize {
    if items == 0usize { return noBucket; }
    let t = hashTag(hash);
    var pos = usize.from(hash) & mask;
    var stride = 0usize;
    while true {
      let g = ctrlGroup(ctrl, pos);
      var m = matchTag(g, t);
      while m != 0u64 {
        let i = (pos + flagByte(m)) & mask;
        if &slots[i].key == key { return i; }
        m = m & (m - 1u64);
      }
      if matchEmpty(g) != 0u64 { return noBucket; }
      stride = stride + 8usize;
      pos = (pos + stride) & mask;
    }
    noBucket
  }

  // One search for an insert: the bucket holding key, if there is one, else the
  // first empty or removed bucket in the probe sequence. The block is made
  // when there is none yet. Whether it found key is 'isFull'
  fn freeOrFound(self: &mut, hash: u64, key: &K) usize {
    if ctrl == null { self.resize(8usize); }
    let t = hashTag(hash);
    var pos = usize.from(hash) & mask;
    var stride = 0usize;
    var spare = noBucket;
    while true {
      let g = ctrlGroup(ctrl, pos);
      var m = matchTag(g, t);
      while m != 0u64 {
        let i = (pos + flagByte(m)) & mask;
        if &slots[i].key == key { return i; }
        m = m & (m - 1u64);
      }
      if spare == noBucket {
        let free = matchFree(g);
        if free != 0u64 { spare = (pos + flagByte(free)) & mask; }
      }
      if matchEmpty(g) != 0u64 { return spare; }
      stride = stride + 8usize;
      pos = (pos + stride) & mask;
    }
    spare
  }

  // The bucket a new entry goes in, given the free one the search found: the
  // table grows first (or is rebuilt at its size, when removed buckets are most
  // of what fills it) if the bucket is an empty one and none are left to fill
  fn roomFor(self: &mut, hash: u64, spare: usize) usize {
    if left > 0usize || mem.readRaw[u8](ctrl + spare) != ctrlEmpty { return spare; }
    let wanted = items + 1usize;
    let buckets = mask + 1usize;
    self.resize(if wanted <= bucketsCapacity(buckets) / 2usize {buckets} else {capacityBuckets(wanted)});
    freeBucket(ctrl, mask, hash)
  }

  // Move an entry into bucket i, which is free
  fn fill(self: &mut, i: usize, hash: u64, key: K, value: V) {
    if mem.readRaw[u8](ctrl + i) == ctrlEmpty { left = left - 1usize; }
    ctrlSet(ctrl, mask, i, hashTag(hash));
    items = items + 1usize;
    mem.writeRaw(slots + i, DictEntry[K, V](key, value));
  }

  // Remove the entry in bucket i: its key is finalized, its value moved out.
  // The bucket becomes empty if no search can have gone past it (a full group
  // around it that has an empty bucket on one side), else removed
  fn take(self: &mut, i: usize) Option[V] {
    if ctrlErase(ctrl, mask, i) { left = left + 1usize; }
    items = items - 1usize;
    // The value is read out of its bucket raw, since nothing moves out of a
    // field, and the key is finalized where it sits
    let slot = (slots + i) as &mut DictEntry[K, V];
    mem.finalize(&slot.key as *K);
    Some[V](mem.readRaw(&slot.value as *V))
  }

  // The bytes of the block of this many buckets: the entries, then the control
  // bytes and their copy of the first eight
  fn tableBytes(self: &, buckets: usize) usize {
    buckets * mem.sizeof[DictEntry[K, V]]() + buckets + 8usize
  }

  // A new block of this many buckets, every entry moved into it by its hash
  fn resize(self: &mut, buckets: usize) {
    let entrySize = mem.sizeof[DictEntry[K, V]]();
    let base = (region as &MemRegion).allocate(self.tableBytes(buckets), mem.alignof[DictEntry[K, V]]());
    let fresh = base + buckets * entrySize;
    libc.memset(fresh, 255i32, buckets + 8usize);
    let freshSlots = base as *DictEntry[K, V];
    let freshMask = buckets - 1usize;
    if ctrl != null {
      var i = 0usize;
      while i <= mask {
        if self.isFull(i) {
          let hash = self.hashOf(&slots[i].key);
          let j = freeBucket(fresh, freshMask, hash);
          ctrlSet(fresh, freshMask, j, hashTag(hash));
          mem.moveRaw(freshSlots + j, slots + i, 1usize);
        }
        i = i + 1usize;
      }
      (region as &MemRegion).release(slots as *u8, self.tableBytes(mask + 1usize));
    }
    ctrl = fresh;
    slots = freshSlots;
    mask = freshMask;
    left = bucketsCapacity(buckets) - items;
  }

  // Its death: each entry's key and value, first bucket to last, then the block,
  // given back to its region
  fn final(self: &uni) {
    if ctrl != null {
      if mem.needsFinal[DictEntry[K, V]]() {
        var i = 0usize;
        while i <= mask {
          if mem.readRaw[u8](ctrl + i) < ctrlRemoved { mem.finalize(slots + i); }
          i = i + 1usize;
        }
      }
      (region as &MemRegion).release(slots as *u8, self.tableBytes(mask + 1usize));
    }
  }
}

// The next full bucket at or after pos, or noBucket
fn nextFull(ctrl: *u8, mask: usize, pos: usize) usize {
  if ctrl == null { return noBucket; }
  var i = pos;
  while i <= mask {
    if mem.readRaw[u8](ctrl + i) < ctrlRemoved { return i; }
    i = i + 1usize;
  }
  noBucket
}

// A cursor over a dictionary's keys: 'next' gives a borrow of each in turn.
// It borrows the dictionary, which may not change while the cursor, or a key
// it gave, is used
pub struct DictKeys['a, K: Hash, V] is Iterator[&K] {
  dict: &'a Dict[K, V];
  pos: usize;

  pub fn next(self: &mut) Option[&'a K] {
    let i = nextFull(dict.ctrl, dict.mask, pos);
    if i == noBucket { pos = dict.mask + 1usize; return None; }
    pos = i + 1usize;
    Some[&K](&dict.slots[i].key)
  }
}

// A cursor over a dictionary's values
pub struct DictValues['a, K: Hash, V] is Iterator[&V] {
  dict: &'a Dict[K, V];
  pos: usize;

  pub fn next(self: &mut) Option[&'a V] {
    let i = nextFull(dict.ctrl, dict.mask, pos);
    if i == noBucket { pos = dict.mask + 1usize; return None; }
    pos = i + 1usize;
    Some[&V](&dict.slots[i].value)
  }
}

// A cursor over a dictionary's values for a collector to trace them: 'next'
// gives a writable reference to each. Keys are not walked: a collector that
// moved one would leave it hashed where it was, so a key that holds a traced
// reference is refused
pub struct DictGcTraceItems['a, K: Hash, V] is Iterator[&mut V] {
  dict: &'a mut Dict[K, V];
  pos: usize;

  pub fn next(self: &mut) Option[&'a mut V] {
    let i = nextFull(dict.ctrl, dict.mask, pos);
    if i == noBucket { pos = dict.mask + 1usize; return None; }
    pos = i + 1usize;
    Some[&mut V](&mut dict.slots[i].value)
  }
}

// A cursor over a dictionary's entries: a borrow of each key with its value
pub struct DictEntries['a, K: Hash, V] is Iterator[&DictEntry[K, V]] {
  dict: &'a Dict[K, V];
  pos: usize;

  pub fn next(self: &mut) Option[&'a DictEntry[K, V]] {
    let i = nextFull(dict.ctrl, dict.mask, pos);
    if i == noBucket { pos = dict.mask + 1usize; return None; }
    pos = i + 1usize;
    Some[&DictEntry[K, V]]((dict.slots + i) as &DictEntry[K, V])
  }
}

// A cursor over a dictionary's pairs: a borrow of each key with a borrow of its
// value, as a tuple
pub struct DictPairs['a, K: Hash, V] is Iterator[(&K, &V)] {
  dict: &'a Dict[K, V];
  pos: usize;

  pub fn next(self: &mut) Option[(&'a K, &'a V)] {
    let i = nextFull(dict.ctrl, dict.mask, pos);
    if i == noBucket { pos = dict.mask + 1usize; return None; }
    pos = i + 1usize;
    Some[(&K, &V)]((&dict.slots[i].key, &dict.slots[i].value))
  }
}

// A cursor over a dictionary's pairs, to change the values in place: a borrow of
// each key to read with a '&mut' borrow of its value, as a tuple
pub struct DictMutPairs['a, K: Hash, V] is Iterator[(&K, &mut V)] {
  dict: &'a mut Dict[K, V];
  pos: usize;

  pub fn next(self: &mut) Option[(&'a K, &'a mut V)] {
    let i = nextFull(dict.ctrl, dict.mask, pos);
    if i == noBucket { pos = dict.mask + 1usize; return None; }
    pos = i + 1usize;
    Some[(&K, &mut V)]((&dict.slots[i].key, &mut dict.slots[i].value))
  }
}

// -------- OrderedDict --------

// A dictionary that visits its entries in the order they were first inserted:
// the layout of Python's compact dict and Rust's IndexMap. Two blocks. The
// entries are dense, one array of (hash, key, value) in insertion order, and
// 'keys', 'values' and 'entries' walk it front to back, with no empty buckets
// to skip. Beside it is an index, a SwissTable's control bytes (the same bytes,
// the same eight-at-a-time probe and the same 7-in-8 fullness as Dict's) and,
// for each bucket, a u32 that is the position of its entry in the array. A
// lookup probes the control bytes, reads the position, and compares the key
// there: one hop more than Dict's, which keeps its entries in the buckets
// themselves.
//
// The order is the point, so it does not change except as the entries do:
// inserting a key it already holds replaces that entry in its place, and
// removing one closes the gap, every later entry moving down one and its index
// fixed (IndexMap's shift_remove). That makes 'remove' cost the entries after
// the removed one; 'swapRemove' is O(1) and moves the last entry into the gap,
// which breaks the order, and is named for it. The index is a u32, so a table
// holds fewer than 2^32 entries.
//
// Everything else is Dict's: K is any Hash type, a key lookup takes a '&K', an
// OrderedDict[String, V] takes the bytes of a key too, each has a seed of its
// own ('empty' the process's, 'deterministic' a fixed one; the order of
// iteration does not depend on it, only which bucket a key is in), the block
// starts at 8 buckets (7 entries), and a borrow from a lookup or a cursor is
// good only until the table next changes.

// One entry: the hash of its key, as the dictionary computed it, kept so that
// growing never hashes again, and the key and the value
pub struct OrderedEntry[K, V] {
  pub hash: u64;
  pub key: K;
  pub value: V;
}

pub struct OrderedDict[K: Hash, V] is ShapeChanging, Sendable, CarriesRegion, GcTraceElements {
  ctrl: *u8;                        // the control bytes, past the index in its block
  index: *u32;                      // per bucket, its entry's position; live only where ctrl is full
  dense: *OrderedEntry[K, V];       // the entries in order, 'count' live, room for 7/8 of the buckets
  mask: usize;                      // the buckets, less one
  count: usize;                     // how many entries
  left: usize;                      // how many more empty buckets may be filled before it is rebuilt
  hasher: Hasher;
  region: *MemRegion;                  // where both blocks come from and go back to

  // An empty dictionary, 'OrderedDict[String, i64]()': no block until the
  // first entry arrives. The last argument of every init is the region the
  // blocks will come from (see List)
  pub fn initEmpty overload init(self: &new, region: *MemRegion) {
    *self = OrderedDict(null, null, null, 0usize, 0usize, 0usize, Hasher.seeded(), region);
  }

  // An empty dictionary whose blocks already have room for n entries without
  // growing (rounded up to the table's size), 'OrderedDict[String, i64](100)'
  pub fn initCapacity overload init(self: &new, n: usize, region: *MemRegion) {
    var d = OrderedDict(null, null, null, 0usize, 0usize, 0usize, Hasher.seeded(), region);
    if n > 0usize { d.resize(capacityBuckets(n)); }
    *self = d;
  }

  pub fn empty() OrderedDict { OrderedDict(null, null, null, 0usize, 0usize, 0usize, Hasher.seeded(), heapRegion()) }

  // An empty dictionary whose blocks already have room for n entries without growing
  pub fn withCapacity(n: usize) OrderedDict {
    var d = OrderedDict(null, null, null, 0usize, 0usize, 0usize, Hasher.seeded(), heapRegion());
    if n > 0usize { d.resize(capacityBuckets(n)); }
    d
  }

  // An empty dictionary with a fixed seed
  pub fn deterministic() OrderedDict { OrderedDict.withSeed(fixedSeed) }

  // An empty dictionary with this seed: a key hashes to what 'Hasher.withSeed(seed)'
  // makes of what it feeds
  pub fn withSeed(seed: u64) OrderedDict {
    OrderedDict(null, null, null, 0usize, 0usize, 0usize, Hasher.withSeed(seed), heapRegion())
  }

  // How many entries it holds, and how many it holds before it next grows
  pub fn len(self: &) usize { count }
  pub fn capacity(self: &) usize { if ctrl == null {0usize} else {bucketsCapacity(mask + 1usize)} }
  pub fn isEmpty(self: &) bool { count == 0usize }

  // Move key and value in, at the end. A key it already holds has its entry
  // replaced where it stands: the old key is finalized and the old value moved
  // out to the caller, as an Option; None when the key was new.
  pub fn insert(self: &mut, key: K, value: V) Option[V] {
    let hash = self.hashOf(&key);
    let found = self.freeOrFound(hash, &key);
    if self.isFull(found) {
      let at = dense +self.posOf(found);
      let slot = at as &mut OrderedEntry[K, V];
      mem.finalize(&slot.key as *K);
      let old = mem.readRaw(&slot.value as *V);
      mem.writeRaw(at, OrderedEntry[K, V](hash, key, value));
      return Some[V](old);
    }
    self.fill(self.roomFor(hash, found), hash, key, value);
    None
  }

  // 'dict <- key: value' is 'dict.insert(key, value)'
  pub fn insertPair overload `<-`(self: &mut, key: K, value: V) { self.insert(key, value); }

  // A borrow of key's value for changing it in place: the value already there,
  // or else the one given, moved in at the end, with one search
  pub fn getOrInsert(self: &mut, key: K, value: V) &mut V {
    let hash = self.hashOf(&key);
    let found = self.freeOrFound(hash, &key);
    if self.isFull(found) { return &mut dense[self.posOf(found)].value; }
    let p = self.fill(self.roomFor(hash, found), hash, key, value);
    &mut dense[p].value
  }

  // The lookups, as Dict's are: each takes a '&K', the key itself, and, for a
  // String, Uni[str] or Rc[str] key, a '&str', the body it lends or holds (the
  // ones ending in Text; callers write the plain name)

  // Whether it holds key
  pub fn containsKey overload contains(self: &, key: &K) bool { self.find(self.hashOf(key), key) != noBucket }
  pub fn containsText overload contains(self: &, key: &str) bool where K is String || K is Uni[str] || K is Rc[str] {
    self.find(self.hashOf(key), key) != noBucket
  }

  // A borrow of key's value, for reading it now, or None
  pub fn getKey overload get(self: &, key: &K) Option[&V] {
    let b = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if b == noBucket { return None; }
    Some[&V](&dense[self.posOf(b)].value)
  }
  pub fn getText overload get(self: &, key: &str) Option[&V] where K is String || K is Uni[str] || K is Rc[str] {
    let b = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if b == noBucket { return None; }
    Some[&V](&dense[self.posOf(b)].value)
  }

  // A borrow of key's value for changing it in place, or None
  pub fn getMutKey overload getMut(self: &mut, key: &K) Option[&mut V] {
    let b = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if b == noBucket { return None; }
    Some[&mut V](&mut dense[self.posOf(b)].value)
  }
  pub fn getMutText overload getMut(self: &mut, key: &str) Option[&mut V] where K is String || K is Uni[str] || K is Rc[str] {
    let b = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if b == noBucket { return None; }
    Some[&mut V](&mut dense[self.posOf(b)].value)
  }

  // A copy of key's value, or None: the lookup that holds no borrow into the
  // table, so it works through a shared owner, where V copies
  pub fn getCopyKey overload getCopy(self: &, key: &K) Option[V] where V is Copy {
    let b = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if b == noBucket { return None; }
    Some[V](dense[self.posOf(b)].value)
  }
  pub fn getCopyText overload getCopy(self: &, key: &str) Option[V] where (K is String || K is Uni[str] || K is Rc[str]) && V is Copy {
    let b = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if b == noBucket { return None; }
    Some[V](dense[self.posOf(b)].value)
  }

  // 'dict[&key]': a borrow of key's value; a key it does not hold is a panic
  // at the line of the caller (naming the key, when it is given as text)
  pub fn indexKey overload `[]`(self: &, key: &K, file: &Array[u8] = srcFile(), line: u32 = srcLine()) &V {
    let b = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if b == noBucket { panic("collections: OrderedDict has no entry for the key", file, line); }
    &dense[self.posOf(b)].value
  }
  pub fn indexText overload `[]`(self: &, key: &str, file: &Array[u8] = srcFile(), line: u32 = srcLine()) &V where K is String || K is Uni[str] || K is Rc[str] {
    let b = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if b == noBucket { missingKey(key, file, line); }
    &dense[self.posOf(b)].value
  }

  // '&mut dict[&key]': a borrow of key's value for changing it, a panic as above
  pub fn indexMutKey overload `&[]`(self: &mut, key: &K, file: &Array[u8] = srcFile(), line: u32 = srcLine()) &mut V {
    let b = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if b == noBucket { panic("collections: OrderedDict has no entry for the key", file, line); }
    &mut dense[self.posOf(b)].value
  }
  pub fn indexMutText overload `&[]`(self: &mut, key: &str, file: &Array[u8] = srcFile(), line: u32 = srcLine()) &mut V where K is String || K is Uni[str] || K is Rc[str] {
    let b = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if b == noBucket { missingKey(key, file, line); }
    &mut dense[self.posOf(b)].value
  }

  // Move key's value out and finalize the key it was held under, or None when
  // it holds no such key. Every entry after it moves down one place, so the
  // others keep their order
  pub fn removeKey overload remove(self: &mut, key: &K) Option[V] {
    let b = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if b == noBucket { return None; }
    Some[V](self.takeShift(b))
  }
  pub fn removeText overload remove(self: &mut, key: &str) Option[V] where K is String || K is Uni[str] || K is Rc[str] {
    let b = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if b == noBucket { return None; }
    Some[V](self.takeShift(b))
  }

  // The same, but the last entry takes the removed one's place instead of the
  // later ones all moving down: O(1), and the order is no longer the order of
  // insertion
  pub fn swapRemoveKey overload swapRemove(self: &mut, key: &K) Option[V] {
    let b = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if b == noBucket { return None; }
    Some[V](self.takeSwap(b))
  }
  pub fn swapRemoveText overload swapRemove(self: &mut, key: &str) Option[V] where K is String || K is Uni[str] || K is Rc[str] {
    let b = self.find(self.hashOf(key), key);
    if b == noBucket { return None; }
    Some[V](self.takeSwap(b))
  }

  // Finalize every key and value, first inserted to last, and keep the blocks:
  // it holds nothing, with the room it had
  pub fn clear(self: &mut) {
    if ctrl == null { return; }
    if mem.needsFinal[OrderedEntry[K, V]]() {
      var i = 0usize;
      while i < count {
        mem.finalize(dense +i);
        i = i + 1usize;
      }
    }
    libc.memset(ctrl, 255i32, mask + 1usize + 8usize);
    count = 0usize;
    left = bucketsCapacity(mask + 1usize);
  }

  // Cursors over what it holds, each giving a borrow into the table for reading
  // now, with 'next' until it answers None, in the order of insertion. The
  // table may not change while one is used.
  pub fn keys(self: &) OrderedKeys[K, V] { OrderedKeys[K, V](self, 0usize) }
  pub fn values(self: &) OrderedValues[K, V] { OrderedValues[K, V](self, 0usize) }
  pub fn entries(self: &) OrderedEntries[K, V] { OrderedEntries[K, V](self, 0usize) }

  // How a collector reaches what the dictionary holds, in its dense block: a
  // writable reference to each value, in the order of insertion (see Dict.gcTraceElements)
  pub fn gcTraceElements(self: &mut) OrderedDictGcTraceItems[K, V] { OrderedDictGcTraceItems[K, V](self, 0usize) }

  // A cursor over its pairs, which 'each k, v in dict' walks: 'next' gives a
  // pair of borrows, a key and its value, in the order of insertion
  pub fn iter(self: &) OrderedPairs[K, V] { OrderedPairs[K, V](self, 0usize) }

  // The cursor a written '&mut' walks by (see Dict.viewMut)
  pub fn viewMut(self: &mut) OrderedMutPairs[K, V] { OrderedMutPairs[K, V](self, 0usize) }

  // The hash of a key: the key fed to this dictionary's hasher
  fn hashOf[B](self: &, key: &B) u64 {
    var h = hasher;
    (*key).hash(&mut h);
    h.finish()
  }

  // Whether the bucket holds an entry (its control byte's top bit is clear)
  fn isFull(self: &, b: usize) bool { mem.readRaw[u8](ctrl + b) < ctrlRemoved }

  // The position in 'dense' of the entry the full bucket b names
  fn posOf(self: &, b: usize) usize { usize.from(mem.readRaw[u32](index + b)) }

  // The bucket naming the entry that holds key, or noBucket
  fn find[B](self: &, hash: u64, key: &B) usize {
    if count == 0usize { return noBucket; }
    let t = hashTag(hash);
    var pos = usize.from(hash) & mask;
    var stride = 0usize;
    while true {
      let g = ctrlGroup(ctrl, pos);
      var m = matchTag(g, t);
      while m != 0u64 {
        let b = (pos + flagByte(m)) & mask;
        if &dense[self.posOf(b)].key == key { return b; }
        m = m & (m - 1u64);
      }
      if matchEmpty(g) != 0u64 { return noBucket; }
      stride = stride + 8usize;
      pos = (pos + stride) & mask;
    }
    noBucket
  }

  // The bucket naming the entry at position p, whose key hashes to hash. The
  // entry is there, so the search finds it
  fn bucketAt(self: &, hash: u64, p: usize) usize {
    let t = hashTag(hash);
    var pos = usize.from(hash) & mask;
    var stride = 0usize;
    while true {
      let g = ctrlGroup(ctrl, pos);
      var m = matchTag(g, t);
      while m != 0u64 {
        let b = (pos + flagByte(m)) & mask;
        if self.posOf(b) == p { return b; }
        m = m & (m - 1u64);
      }
      if matchEmpty(g) != 0u64 { unreachable("collections: OrderedDict index lost an entry"); }
      stride = stride + 8usize;
      pos = (pos + stride) & mask;
    }
    noBucket
  }

  // One search for an insert: the bucket naming key's entry, if there is one,
  // else the first empty or removed bucket in the probe sequence. The blocks
  // are made when there are none yet. Whether it found key is 'isFull'
  fn freeOrFound(self: &mut, hash: u64, key: &K) usize {
    if ctrl == null { self.resize(8usize); }
    let t = hashTag(hash);
    var pos = usize.from(hash) & mask;
    var stride = 0usize;
    var spare = noBucket;
    while true {
      let g = ctrlGroup(ctrl, pos);
      var m = matchTag(g, t);
      while m != 0u64 {
        let b = (pos + flagByte(m)) & mask;
        if &dense[self.posOf(b)].key == key { return b; }
        m = m & (m - 1u64);
      }
      if spare == noBucket {
        let free = matchFree(g);
        if free != 0u64 { spare = (pos + flagByte(free)) & mask; }
      }
      if matchEmpty(g) != 0u64 { return spare; }
      stride = stride + 8usize;
      pos = (pos + stride) & mask;
    }
    spare
  }

  // The bucket a new entry goes in, given the free one the search found: the
  // index is rebuilt first (grown, or at its size when removed buckets are
  // most of what fills it) if the bucket is an empty one and none are left
  // to fill. The entries array has room whenever the index does
  fn roomFor(self: &mut, hash: u64, spare: usize) usize {
    if left > 0usize || mem.readRaw[u8](ctrl + spare) != ctrlEmpty { return spare; }
    let wanted = count + 1usize;
    let buckets = mask + 1usize;
    self.resize(if wanted <= bucketsCapacity(buckets) / 2usize {buckets} else {capacityBuckets(wanted)});
    freeBucket(ctrl, mask, hash)
  }

  // Append an entry, named by the free bucket b. It answers its position
  fn fill(self: &mut, b: usize, hash: u64, key: K, value: V) usize {
    if mem.readRaw[u8](ctrl + b) == ctrlEmpty { left = left - 1usize; }
    ctrlSet(ctrl, mask, b, hashTag(hash));
    let p = count;
    mem.writeRaw[u32](index + b, u32.from(p));
    mem.writeRaw(dense +p, OrderedEntry[K, V](hash, key, value));
    count = p + 1usize;
    p
  }

  // Make bucket b, which names a live entry, free
  fn release(self: &mut, b: usize) {
    if ctrlErase(ctrl, mask, b) { left = left + 1usize; }
  }

  // Remove the entry bucket b names, closing the gap: its key is finalized,
  // its value moved out, the later entries move down one place, and each one's
  // bucket is told. A short tail is found one entry at a time by hash; a long
  // one by reading the whole index
  fn takeShift(self: &mut, b: usize) V {
    let p = self.posOf(b);
    self.release(b);
    let slot = (dense +p) as &mut OrderedEntry[K, V];
    mem.finalize(&slot.key as *K);
    let value = mem.readRaw(&slot.value as *V);
    count = count - 1usize;
    let moved = count - p;
    if moved > 0usize {
      mem.moveRaw(dense +p, dense +p + 1usize, moved);
      if moved * 8usize < mask + 1usize {
        var q = p;
        while q < count {
          mem.writeRaw[u32](index + self.bucketAt(dense[q].hash, q + 1usize), u32.from(q));
          q = q + 1usize;
        }
      }
      else {
        var i = 0usize;
        while i <= mask {
          if self.isFull(i) && self.posOf(i) > p {
            mem.writeRaw[u32](index + i, u32.from(self.posOf(i) - 1usize));
          }
          i = i + 1usize;
        }
      }
    }
    value
  }

  // Remove the entry bucket b names, the last entry taking its place
  fn takeSwap(self: &mut, b: usize) V {
    let p = self.posOf(b);
    self.release(b);
    let slot = (dense +p) as &mut OrderedEntry[K, V];
    mem.finalize(&slot.key as *K);
    let value = mem.readRaw(&slot.value as *V);
    count = count - 1usize;
    if p != count {
      let lastBucket = self.bucketAt(dense[count].hash, count);
      mem.moveRaw(dense +p, dense +count, 1usize);
      mem.writeRaw[u32](index + lastBucket, u32.from(p));
    }
    value
  }

  // A new index of this many buckets (a power of two, 8 or more) over the same
  // entries, in the same order; the entries array grows with it
  fn resize(self: &mut, buckets: usize) {
    if buckets > 4294967296usize { panic("collections: OrderedDict is past 2^32 buckets", srcFile(), srcLine()); }
    let base = (region as &MemRegion).allocate(buckets * 5usize + 8usize, mem.alignof[u32]());
    let fresh = base + buckets * 4usize;
    libc.memset(fresh, 255i32, buckets + 8usize);
    let freshIndex = base as *u32;
    let freshMask = buckets - 1usize;
    var i = 0usize;
    while i < count {
      let hash = dense[i].hash;
      let j = freeBucket(fresh, freshMask, hash);
      ctrlSet(fresh, freshMask, j, hashTag(hash));
      mem.writeRaw[u32](freshIndex + j, u32.from(i));
      i = i + 1usize;
    }
    let oldCap = if ctrl == null {0usize} else {bucketsCapacity(mask + 1usize)};
    let newCap = bucketsCapacity(buckets);
    if newCap != oldCap {
      dense = blockGrow[OrderedEntry[K, V]](region, dense, oldCap, newCap);
    }
    if ctrl != null { (region as &MemRegion).release(index as *u8, (mask + 1usize) * 5usize + 8usize); }
    ctrl = fresh;
    index = freshIndex;
    mask = freshMask;
    left = newCap - count;
  }

  // Its death: each entry's key and value, first inserted to last, then the
  // blocks, given back to its region
  fn final(self: &uni) {
    if ctrl != null {
      if mem.needsFinal[OrderedEntry[K, V]]() {
        var i = 0usize;
        while i < count {
          mem.finalize(dense +i);
          i = i + 1usize;
        }
      }
      blockFree[OrderedEntry[K, V]](region, dense, bucketsCapacity(mask + 1usize));
      (region as &MemRegion).release(index as *u8, (mask + 1usize) * 5usize + 8usize);
    }
  }
}

// A cursor over an OrderedDict's keys: 'next' gives a borrow of each in turn,
// in the order of insertion. It borrows the dictionary, which may not change
// while the cursor, or a key it gave, is used
pub struct OrderedKeys['a, K: Hash, V] is Iterator[&K] {
  dict: &'a OrderedDict[K, V];
  pos: usize;

  pub fn next(self: &mut) Option[&'a K] {
    if pos >= dict.count { return None; }
    let i = pos;
    pos = i + 1usize;
    Some[&K](&dict.dense[i].key)
  }
}

// A cursor over an OrderedDict's values, in the order of insertion
pub struct OrderedValues['a, K: Hash, V] is Iterator[&V] {
  dict: &'a OrderedDict[K, V];
  pos: usize;

  pub fn next(self: &mut) Option[&'a V] {
    if pos >= dict.count { return None; }
    let i = pos;
    pos = i + 1usize;
    Some[&V](&dict.dense[i].value)
  }
}

// A cursor over an OrderedDict's values for a collector to trace them: 'next'
// gives a writable reference to each, in the order of insertion (see DictGcTraceItems)
pub struct OrderedDictGcTraceItems['a, K: Hash, V] is Iterator[&mut V] {
  dict: &'a mut OrderedDict[K, V];
  pos: usize;

  pub fn next(self: &mut) Option[&'a mut V] {
    if pos >= dict.count { return None; }
    let i = pos;
    pos = i + 1usize;
    Some[&mut V](&mut dict.dense[i].value)
  }
}

// A cursor over an OrderedDict's entries: a borrow of each key with its value
// (and its hash), in the order of insertion
pub struct OrderedEntries['a, K: Hash, V] is Iterator[&OrderedEntry[K, V]] {
  dict: &'a OrderedDict[K, V];
  pos: usize;

  pub fn next(self: &mut) Option[&'a OrderedEntry[K, V]] {
    if pos >= dict.count { return None; }
    let i = pos;
    pos = i + 1usize;
    Some[&OrderedEntry[K, V]]((dict.dense +i) as &OrderedEntry[K, V])
  }
}

// A cursor over an OrderedDict's pairs: a borrow of each key with a borrow of
// its value, as a tuple, in the order of insertion
pub struct OrderedPairs['a, K: Hash, V] is Iterator[(&K, &V)] {
  dict: &'a OrderedDict[K, V];
  pos: usize;

  pub fn next(self: &mut) Option[(&'a K, &'a V)] {
    if pos >= dict.count { return None; }
    let i = pos;
    pos = i + 1usize;
    Some[(&K, &V)]((&dict.dense[i].key, &dict.dense[i].value))
  }
}

// A cursor over an OrderedDict's pairs, to change the values in place, in the
// order of insertion (see DictMutPairs)
pub struct OrderedMutPairs['a, K: Hash, V] is Iterator[(&K, &mut V)] {
  dict: &'a mut OrderedDict[K, V];
  pos: usize;

  pub fn next(self: &mut) Option[(&'a K, &'a mut V)] {
    if pos >= dict.count { return None; }
    let i = pos;
    pos = i + 1usize;
    Some[(&K, &mut V)]((&dict.dense[i].key, &mut dict.dense[i].value))
  }
}

// -------- Set --------

// A set of T, unordered: a Dict of T to nothing, so it has the Dict's table, its
// seed and its rules. T is any Hash type; a Set[String] also takes an element
// as the '&str' it lends. Its elements are visited in the table's order, which is the
// seed's: sort them, or ask 'contains', and never rely on it. A borrow from a
// cursor is good only until the set next changes.

// What a Set's dictionary holds beside each element: nothing, in no bytes
struct Member {}

pub struct Set[T: Hash] is ShapeChanging, Sendable, CarriesRegion {
  dict: Dict[T, Member];

  // An empty set, 'Set[String]()'. Its seed is the process's. The last
  // argument of every init is the region the block will come from (see List),
  // which the set hands its dictionary
  pub fn initEmpty overload init(self: &new, region: *MemRegion) { *self = Set(Dict[T, Member](region)); }

  // An empty set whose block already has room for n elements without growing
  // (rounded up to the table's size), 'Set[String](100)'
  pub fn initCapacity overload init(self: &new, n: usize, region: *MemRegion) {
    *self = Set(Dict[T, Member](n, region));
  }

  pub fn empty() Set { Set(Dict[T, Member].empty()) }

  // An empty set whose block already has room for n elements without growing
  pub fn withCapacity(n: usize) Set { Set(Dict[T, Member].withCapacity(n)) }

  // An empty set with a fixed seed: the same elements inserted in the same
  // order are visited in the same order on every run
  pub fn deterministic() Set { Set(Dict[T, Member].deterministic()) }

  // An empty set with this seed
  pub fn withSeed(seed: u64) Set { Set(Dict[T, Member].withSeed(seed)) }

  pub fn len(self: &) usize { dict.len() }
  pub fn capacity(self: &) usize { dict.capacity() }
  pub fn isEmpty(self: &) bool { dict.isEmpty() }

  // Move item in. It answers whether it was new: an element the set already
  // holds stays, and the item given dies
  pub fn insert(self: &mut, item: T) bool {
    let before = dict.len();
    dict.getOrInsert(item, Member());
    dict.len() > before
  }

  // 'set <- item' is 'set.insert(item)'
  pub fn insertItem overload `<-`(self: &mut, item: T) { self.insert(item); }

  // Whether it holds item
  pub fn containsItem overload contains(self: &, item: &T) bool { dict.contains(item) }

  // Remove item, finalizing the element held; whether it was there
  pub fn removeItem overload remove(self: &mut, item: &T) bool {
    match dict.remove(item) {
      case is Some[Member] {true}
      case is None[Member] {false}
    }
  }

  // The same, given a String, Uni[str] or Rc[str] element as a '&str', the body
  // it lends or holds (a literal finds it)
  pub fn containsText overload contains(self: &, item: &str) bool where T is String || T is Uni[str] || T is Rc[str] { dict.contains(item) }

  pub fn removeText overload remove(self: &mut, item: &str) bool where T is String || T is Uni[str] || T is Rc[str] {
    match dict.remove(item) {
      case is Some[Member] {true}
      case is None[Member] {false}
    }
  }

  // Finalize every element and keep the room
  pub fn clear(self: &mut) { dict.clear(); }

  // A cursor over its elements: 'next' gives a borrow of each in turn, in the
  // table's order, until it answers None
  pub fn items(self: &) SetItems[T] { SetItems[T](dict.keys()) }

  // The same cursor under the name 'each x in set' walks it by
  pub fn iter(self: &) SetItems[T] { SetItems[T](dict.keys()) }
}

// A cursor over a set's elements
pub struct SetItems['a, T: Hash] is Iterator[&T] {
  inner: DictKeys['a, T, Member];

  pub fn next(self: &mut) Option[&'a T] { inner.next() }
}

// -------- capability traits --------

// What a collection can do, one small trait for each, so that a function asks
// for exactly the ability it uses. A trait here is met structurally: a type
// fits one by having the methods, whether or not it ever heard of the trait
// (reference, "Traits"), so List, Deque, Dict, OrderedDict and Set declare
// nothing, and a user's own type with the same methods fits too.
//
// A function asks for one as a static bound: 'fn drain[S Stack[i64]](s &mut
// S)', 'fn drain[T, S: Stack[T]](s: &mut S)' or 'fn drain[T, S](s &mut S) where
// S is Stack[T]', called with '&mut list' or '&mut deque' as it is. The bound
// is decided where the function is called and compiled for the type's own
// methods, so nothing is looked up when it runs. The trait is given all its
// type arguments ('[S: Stack]' is refused, there are no associated types yet),
// and a bound decides a parameter before an unsuffixed integer literal takes a
// type of its own. 'Collection', which mentions no element type, is written
// alone: 'fn count[C: Collection](c: &C)'.
//
// The other form is dynamic: a virtual reference, 'fn drain(s &<mut
// Stack[i64])', takes the same arguments through one compiled function and a
// table of the type's methods. Use it for a value whose type is not known where
// the function is compiled; otherwise prefer the bound.
//
// The names an operation has are the same wherever it exists: 'push' and 'pop'
// at the back, 'pushFront' and 'popFront' at the front, 'len', 'isEmpty'.

// Anything that holds a number of values
pub trait Collection {
  pub fn len(self: &) usize;
  pub fn isEmpty(self: &) bool;
}

// Values reached by their position, counted from the front (List, Deque). 'get'
// answers None for a position that is out of range; the indexing '[i]' panics.
pub trait Indexed[T] {
  pub fn get(self: &, index: usize) Option[&T];
}

// Values pushed and popped at one end, the last in the first out (List, Deque,
// at the back). 'peek' borrows the value 'pop' would move out.
pub trait Stack[T] {
  pub fn push(self: &mut, value: T);
  pub fn pop(self: &mut) Option[T];
  pub fn peek(self: &) Option[&T];
}

// Values pushed at the back and popped from the front, the first in the first
// out (Deque)
pub trait Queue[T] {
  pub fn push(self: &mut, value: T);
  pub fn popFront(self: &mut) Option[T];
}

// Values found by a key (Dict, OrderedDict). 'insert' answers the old value,
// moved out, when the key was already there (Rust's HashMap::insert), else
// None; 'remove' moves the value out.
pub trait Map[K, V] {
  pub fn get(self: &, key: &K) Option[&V];
  pub fn insert(self: &mut, key: K, value: V) Option[V];
  pub fn remove(self: &mut, key: &K) Option[V];
  pub fn contains(self: &, key: &K) bool;
}

// -------- errors --------

// What an error value is [WI error-handling, issue 5]. A library returns its own
// typed errors (an enum or struct that declares 'is Error'); an application takes
// them all as an 'AnyError'. The trait is in this package, where String is.
//
//   message()  one line for a person, this error alone: the outermost line of a
//              chain ('readFile notes.txt: No such file or directory').
//   source()   the error that caused this one, or None (the default).
//   report()   the message and each cause under it, one to a line, which 'main'
//              prints for an error that has it. INTERIM: its text format stands
//              until the display design lands, when 'Error' may come to require
//              the display trait (Rust's 'Error: Display'), with 'message()' kept
//              or made a default.
//
// 'source' answers a virtual reference, '&<Error' (a plain '&' to a trait cannot
// be called through): a type with a cause builds it with the type argument,
// 'Some[&<Error](&cause)'.
pub trait Error {
  pub fn message(self: &) String;

  pub fn source(self: &) Option[&<Error] { None }

  // 'message', then 'caused by:' and each cause's message, down the chain
  pub fn report(self: &) String {
    var out = self.message();
    var cur = self.source();
    var more = true;
    while more {
      match cur {
        case let Some(cause) {
          let line = cause.message();
          out.append("\n  caused by: ");
          out.append(line.view());
          cur = cause.source();
        }
        case is None { more = false; }
      }
    }
    out
  }
}

// An error that is only a line of text: where an application has no typed error
// to give ('AnyError.msg("no such mode")')
struct TextError is Error, Sendable {
  line: String;

  pub fn message(self: &) String { line.copy() }
}

// Any error, owned: what an application's functions return where a library's
// typed errors differ ('fn run() Result[Config, AnyError]'). '?' converts any
// error that is Error and Sendable into one with 'from', as it converts into any
// error type, so an application writes no conversions. It holds a 'Uni' of the
// error, so it is Sendable and crosses between actors (anyhow's Send + Sync).
//
// 'addContext' wraps what an AnyError holds in a line of text, so that the line is
// its 'message()' and the error it was is the 'source()' under it; a chain of
// them is a chain of such lines. 'report()' (the Error trait's default, which
// 'main' prints) gives the lines, then the original error's message and each of
// its own causes.
//
// Getting the original error back out is anyhow's: 'holds[E]()' says whether the
// error is an E ('is' is the language's own word for that test), 'downcastRef[E]()'
// lends it as a '&E' and 'downcastMut[E]()' as a '&mut E', or None. They are a
// downcast of the owned trait value ('case let E(e)' on it, which binds a borrow,
// never the owner), and they look through each line of context to the error under
// it, as anyhow's do, so 'e.addContext("...")' still holds what 'e' held.
pub struct AnyError is Error, Sendable {
  inner: Uni[Error + Sendable];

  // Any error as an AnyError: the conversion '?' finds. (A plain generic method
  // named 'from', not an 'overload from': an overload set takes no generic that
  // is bound by a trait. To be revisited with the overload-ranking design.)
  pub fn from[E: Error + Sendable](e: E) AnyError {
    let owner: Uni[Error + Sendable] = Uni[E](e);
    AnyError(owner)
  }

  // An error that is only this line of text
  pub fn msg(line: &str) AnyError { AnyError.from(TextError(String.from(line))) }

  // The outermost line: the latest context, or the error's own message
  pub fn message(self: &) String { inner.message() }

  // What is under the outermost line
  pub fn source(self: &) Option[&<Error] { inner.source() }

  // This error under one more line of context: 'e.addContext("reading the config")'.
  // ('context' is a reserved word, a planned language feature, so this is not
  // named for it.)
  pub fn addContext(self, line: &str) AnyError {
    AnyError.from(ContextError(String.from(line), self))
  }

  // 'addContext', its line made by 'f' when this is called (anyhow's with_context)
  pub fn withContext[F: fn() String](self, f: F) AnyError {
    AnyError.from(ContextError(f(), self))
  }

  // Whether the error, under any lines of context, is an E (anyhow's 'is')
  pub fn holds[E](self: &) bool {
    match self.downcastRef[E]() {
      case is Some {true}
      else {false}
    }
  }

  // The error as an E, lent, under any lines of context; None where it is not
  // one (anyhow's downcast_ref)
  pub fn downcastRef[E](self: &) Option[&E] {
    if let E(e) = inner { return Some[&E](e); }
    if let ContextError(c) = inner { return c.cause.downcastRef[E](); }
    None
  }

  // The same, lent mutably (anyhow's downcast_mut)
  pub fn downcastMut[E](self: &mut) Option[&mut E] {
    if let E(e) = inner { return Some[&mut E](e); }
    if let ContextError(c) = inner { return c.cause.downcastMut[E](); }
    None
  }
}

// An error that is a line of text, and another error under it for 'addContext'.
// (Declared after AnyError, which it holds: written before it, its 'source' is
// refused, the AnyError it lends not yet taken for an Error.)
struct ContextError is Error, Sendable {
  line: String;
  cause: AnyError;

  pub fn message(self: &) String { line.copy() }
  pub fn source(self: &) Option[&<Error] { Some[&<Error](&cause) }
}

// -------- byte slices --------

// The functions the types above read bytes with, public for bytes that are
// not a '&str' (a buffer, a part of one, a binary format), and the number
// readers, which take text.

// Whether a and b hold the same bytes: 'a == b'
pub fn bytesEqual(a: &Array[u8], b: &Array[u8]) bool {
  a == b
}

// -1, 0 or 1 as a sorts before, with or after b
pub fn bytesCompare(a: &Array[u8], b: &Array[u8]) i32 {
  var i = 0usize;
  while i < a.len && i < b.len {
    if a[i] < b[i] { return -1i32; }
    if a[i] > b[i] { return 1i32; }
    i = i + 1usize;
  }
  if a.len < b.len { -1i32 } else if a.len > b.len { 1i32 } else { 0i32 }
}

// Where needle first starts in hay at or after 'from', or None
pub fn bytesFind(hay: &Array[u8], needle: &Array[u8], from: usize) Option[usize] {
  var i = from;
  while i + needle.len <= hay.len {
    if &hay[i..<i + needle.len] == needle { return Some[usize](i); }
    i = i + 1usize;
  }
  None
}

// A space, tab, carriage return or line feed
pub fn isSpace(b: u8) bool { b == ' ' || b == '\t' || b == '\r' || b == '\n' }

// The byte, made small if it is an ASCII capital letter, A to Z
pub fn asciiLower(b: u8) u8 { if b >= 'A' && b <= 'Z' {b + 32u8} else {b} }

// The byte, made a capital if it is an ASCII small letter, a to z
pub fn asciiUpper(b: u8) u8 { if b >= 'a' && b <= 'z' {b - 32u8} else {b} }

// The integer the text spells in decimal: a '-' or a '+' or neither, then one
// or more digits, 0 to 9, and nothing else, no spaces either ('trim' first).
// None for any other text, and for a number beyond i64's range, which is
// -9223372036854775808 to 9223372036854775807.
pub fn parseInt(s: &str) Option[i64] {
  let text: &Array[u8] = s;
  var start = 0usize;
  var negative = false;
  if text.len > 0usize && (text[0usize] == '-' || text[0usize] == '+') {
    negative = text[0usize] == '-';
    start = 1usize;
  }
  // A negative number's magnitude reaches one past the largest i64
  let limit = if negative {9223372036854775808u64} else {9223372036854775807u64};
  match digitsValue(&text[start..], limit) {
    case let Some(m) {
      if !negative {Some[i64](i64.from(m))}
      else if m == 0u64 {Some[i64](0i64)}
      else {Some[i64](-1i64 - i64.from(m - 1u64))}
    }
    case is None {None}
  }
}

// The same for a u64, 0 to 18446744073709551615: a '+' or nothing, then the
// digits. A '-' is None, even before 0.
pub fn parseUInt(s: &str) Option[u64] {
  let text: &Array[u8] = s;
  var start = 0usize;
  if text.len > 0usize && text[0usize] == '+' { start = 1usize; }
  digitsValue(&text[start..], 18446744073709551615u64)
}

// The value of one or more decimal digits and nothing else, or None when
// there are none, when anything else is there, or when it is beyond limit
fn digitsValue(digits: &Array[u8], limit: u64) Option[u64] {
  if digits.len == 0usize { return None; }
  var n = 0u64;
  var i = 0usize;
  while i < digits.len {
    let c = digits[i];
    if c < '0' || c > '9' { return None; }
    let d = u64.from(c - '0');
    // Whether n * 10 + d would pass limit, asked without overflowing
    if n > (limit - d) / 10u64 { return None; }
    n = n * 10u64 + d;
    i = i + 1usize;
  }
  Some[u64](n)
}

// -------- program errors --------

// Each a panic, reported where the method finding it was called

fn outOfRange(index: usize, length: usize, file: &Array[u8], line: u32) Never {
  var msg = String.from("collections: index ");
  msg.appendUInt(u64.from(index));
  msg.append(" is out of range for a length of ");
  msg.appendUInt(u64.from(length));
  panic(msg.view(), file, line)
}

// A lookup by '[]' of a key given as text that is not held: the panic names it
fn missingKey(key: &str, file: &Array[u8], line: u32) Never {
  var msg = String.from("collections: no entry for the key \"");
  msg.append(key);
  msg.append("\"");
  panic(msg.view(), file, line)
}
